###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels
:AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                  rdfs:subClassOf :GenerativeAIMethods ;
                                  rdfs:comment """Autoencoder based Generative Models in unsupervised machine learning efficiently compress and reconstruct input data. The structure of Autoencoders includes two primary components: the Encoder, which compresses data into a latent space, and the Decoder, which reconstructs the data to closely replicate the original input. This process aims to minimize loss, typically measured by metrics such as mean
squared error or cross-entropy. In healthcare, Autoencoder based models have demonstrated their effectiveness in generating synthetic datasets that mimic real patient data. These synthetic datasets are valuable for various purposes, including data augmentation, anomaly detection, and de-identification, enhancing data privacy and utility. This is particularly beneficial in scenarios where patient data is limited, sensitive, or imbalanced. Despite their advantages, training Autoencoders also presents challenges. One significant issue is the potential for overfitting, where the model becomes too specialized to the training data and performs poorly on new data. Techniques such as dropout, regularization, and data augmentation are commonly used to mitigate this problem and improve generalization. Recent advancements in the literature have introduced variations of Autoencoders to enhance their performance. For instance, Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) introduce a probabilistic approach to the latent space, providing better generalization and more robust synthetic data generation. Denoising Autoencoders (DAEs) and Sparse Autoencoders are other variants that improve the model’s ability to handle noise and extract meaningful features from high-dimensional data. These Generative models can handle a wide range of data types, including images, text, time series, audio, video, tabular, and Three Dimensional (3D) data. This versatility makes Autoencoder suitable for diverse medical applications, such as analyzing EHRs and medical imaging. For instance, in EHRs, Autoencoders can identify anomalies that may indicate potential health issues, while in medical imaging, they can help improve image resolution and detect subtle abnormalities.""" .


