###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels
:FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                        rdfs:subClassOf :GenerativeAIMethods ;
                                        rdfs:comment """Federated Learning (FL) based generative models utilize a decentralized machine learning framework to learn from distributed datasets across multiple devices without exchanging data, thereby enhancing privacy and reducing centralization risks, particularly in sensitive sectors like healthcare. The structure of these generative models involves each node training locally on its data (local models), a central aggregator refining a global model with these updates, and synchronization across nodes (global model) to iteratively enhance the model (iterative process) without sharing private data. In healthcare, FL enables privacy-preserving data augmentation, collaborative anomaly detection, and the development of enhanced predictive models by utilizing diverse, multi-institutional data without direct data sharing. These approaches support various data types, including text, images, audio, and time-series data from continuous monitoring devices, facilitating comprehensive training models across healthcare sectors. 
Despite its advantages, Federated Learning also presents several challenges. One major issue is the heterogeneity of data across different nodes, which can lead to model biases and inconsistent performance. To address this, techniques such as federated averaging, differential privacy, and secure multiparty computation have been proposed in the literature. These methods ensure robust model training while maintaining data privacy and security. Moreover, the iterative process of synchronizing local updates with the global model can be computationally intensive and requires efficient communication protocols to minimize latency and bandwidth usage. Techniques like compression algorithms and asynchronous updates have been introduced to mitigate these issues, enhancing the scalability and efficiency of FL systems.""" .


