###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TransformerBasedGenerativeModels
:TransformerBasedGenerativeModels rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                  rdfs:subClassOf :GenerativeAIMethods ;
                                  rdfs:comment "Transformer-based Generative Models, utilized in both supervised and unsupervised machine learning, leverage self-attention mechanisms to generate complex sequences, excelling in tasks like text creation, translation, summarization, and image generation. These models feature an Encoder that processes input data into a higher-dimensional space and a Decoder that produces output sequentially, with both components consisting of stacked layers that perform self-attention and pointwise, fully connected operations, regulated by layer normalization and linked by residual connections. In healthcare, Transformer-based models have shown potential in analyzing patient data, predicting treatment outcomes, and summarizing medical documents. These models can generate realistic synthetic records, supporting data augmentation, anonymization, and handling imbalanced datasets. This is essential for training robust AI models in scenarios with sparse annotated data or where privacy preservation is required. These models excel in processing diverse data types, including text, time series, audio, video, and images. This versatility makes them effective for tasks such as managing EHRs, medical transcription, and generating medical reports from radiographic images. For instance, in EHRs, Transformers can extract and summarize key patient information, while in medical imaging, they can assist in generating detailed reports based on radiographic data. Regardless of their capabilities, training Transformer-based models also presents challenges, particularly in managing computational resources and handling large datasets. Several techniques are explained below and advanced optimizers can enhance training efficiency. Additionally, Transformer Extra Long (Transformer-XL) and Generative Pre-trained Transformers (GPTs) have introduced mechanisms to handle long-term dependencies and scale model sizes without compromising performance." .


