                                                                                                              :PeriodicallyReviewedLogsEvidence .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#BERT/BERT-Gen
<http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#BERT/BERT-Gen> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                                          :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
                                                                                 :HasModality :TextData ;
                                                                                 :UsesTechnique :MLM ,
                                                                                                :NSP ,
                                                                                                :PositionalEmbeddings ,
                                                                                                :SegmentEmbeddings ;
                                                                                 rdfs:comment "Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) leverages deep bidirectional transformers to excel in understanding and generating text for a variety of tasks, including question answering and language inference. It is composed of a multilayer bidirectional transformer encoder and utilizes a Masked Language Modeling (MLM) objective for its pre-training. During this process, it randomly masks words in the input text and trains the model to predict the masked words using the surrounding context. The architecture of BERT, along with its training processes and evaluation metrics, is designed with a strong emphasis on transparency and reproducibility. BERT generally uses learning rates between 0.00001 and 0.00005 for tuning and fine-tuning to achieve optimal performance while ensuring convergence. In Ref. [269], the authors introduced Med-BERT, a contextualized embedding model pre-trained on a structured EHR dataset of 28,490,650 patients. They fine-tuned the model and demonstrated that Med-BERT significantly enhances prediction accuracy in two disease prediction tasks from clinical databases. Notably, they showed that pre-trained Med-BERT achieves impressive performance on tasks with small fine-tuning training sets, increasing the Area Under Curve of the Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUCROC) by over 20% compared to deep learning models without Med-BERT. During pretraining, they used a maximum sequence length of 512 tokens and the default AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of 0.00005 and a dropout rate of 0.1. For fine-tuning, they utilized the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.00001 and a batch size of 32. BERT is highly capable of handling various types of text data, making it suitable for a wide range of NLP tasks, including applications such as document summarization, automated conversation systems, named entity recognition, sentiment analysis, and language translation. It can employ several encoding techniques, such as MLM, Next Sentence Prediction (NSP), Positional embeddings, and Segment embeddings, to enhance performance. In NSP, the model learns to understand the relationship between two sentences, which is beneficial for tasks like question answering and sentence pair classification." .


