###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/AAEs
:AAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Adversarial Autoencoders (AAEs) combine the strengths of autoencoders with adversarial training to enhance the generation of diverse and realistic samples by ensuring the latent space closely follows a specified probability distribution. This approach involves a dual-network architecture: the autoencoder network (comprising an encoder and a decoder) and a discriminative network, which is employed to enforce the latent space’s distribution. AAEs typically employ learning rates between 0.0001 and 0.1 for balancing the convergence speed and stability of the training process, such as authors employed Adam optimizer with learning rates 0.01 and batch size 64 for MNIST and CIFAR-10 datasets and learning rate 0.1 with batch size 32 for ImageNet to generate adversarial perturbations on the training data together with one imaginary victim differentiable classifier. Moreover, authors employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0002 and a batch size of 100 to detect abnormal conditions based on anomaly score among patterns. These authors also demonstrated that various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, can be utilized to effectively minimize the reconstruction loss of the autoencoder and the adversarial loss of the discriminator. AAEs incorporate a regularization term in their loss function, which acts as a learned prior. This term facilitates the alignment of latent space distribution of the encoded data with a predefined prior distribution, such as a Gaussian distribution. The loss function typically combines reconstruction loss (mean squared error or crossentropy) with adversarial loss, ensuring that the latent space representations are both meaningful and realistic." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ACGAN
:ACGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :AdversarialAutoencoders ,
                      :BatchNormalisation ,
                      :FeatureMatching ,
                      :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ,
                      :OneHotEncoding ;
       rdfs:comment "The Auxiliary Classifier GAN (ACGAN) enhances traditional GANs by incorporating an auxiliary classifier within its discriminator. This addition allows the model to not only differentiate between real and generated images but also classify the images into specific categories. This dual functionality enables ACGAN to produce high-quality, category-specific synthetic images, such as varied medical scans, thereby improving the quality and utility of synthetic healthcare datasets. ACGAN is designed with a strong focus on transparency and reproducibility, providing well-documented methodologies detailing its architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics. Also it was employed several learning rates between 0.0001 and 0.0004 for stabilizing the training process and ensuring convergence, with a batch size range from 64 to 128 to balance the computational efficiency and model performance, and utilized Adam optimizer to minimize the loss function. Moreover, various optimizers, including Adam, Root Mean Square Propagation (RMSprop), and Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD), can also be used to minimize the loss functions of ACGAN effectively, as well as ACGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ADGAN
:ADGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :AdversarialAutoencoders ,
                      :BatchNormalisation ,
                      :FeatureMatching ,
                      :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ,
                      :NoiseInjection ,
                      :SemanticHashing ;
       rdfs:comment "Attention Directed GAN (ADGAN) leverages attention mechanisms within its architecture to emphasize critical data features during the generation process, thereby enhancing the realism and utility of synthesized datasets, particularly in complex medical imaging. By incorporating attention mechanisms in both the generator and discriminator, ADGAN can produce highly detailed and accurate medical images, enabling precise differentiation of disease stages and subtle distinctions between healthy and diseased tissues. ADGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data. ADGAN’s methodology is comprehensively documented, detailing the model architecture, data sources, training procedures, and evaluation metrics. The training process includes meticulous data preprocessing, setting specific hyperparameters, and employing advanced optimization techniques to ensure robust model performance. The authors in use the learning rate 0.0002 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence with batch size 64 to maintain a balance between computational efficiency and model performance and employ Adam optimizer to effectively minimize the loss functions." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ADM
:ADM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :ImageData ;
     :UsesTechnique :AnatomicalMasks ,
                    :BatchNormalisation ,
                    :DenoisingDiffusion ;
     rdfs:comment "Adversarial Diffusion Models (ADM) combine the capabilities of generative adversarial networks (GANs) and Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) to generate high-quality synthetic data. These models aim to leverage the fidelity and diversity of diffusion models while utilizing the efficient sampling and adversarial training of GANs. The primary purpose of ADMs is to create synthetic datasets that maintain high image quality and diversity. These are particularly useful in domains like medical imaging, where data privacy and availability are significant concerns. ADMs are employed to improve the robustness of adversarially trained models, enhance data synthesis for clinical research, and perform domain translation to create synthetic datasets that can be used for training deep learning algorithms without compromising patient privacy. To address the challenge of improving Adversarial Training (AT) using advanced diffusion models, authors explored whether utilizing recent and more efficient diffusion models can further enhance the robustness and performance of adversarially trained models compared to older models like DDPM. They use the Elucidating Diffusion Model (EDM) to generate synthetic training data and compare it against models trained with data generated by older DDPM models. They integrate EDM-generated data into the adversarial training pipeline and evaluate the model’s performance. They employed an SGD optimizer with a learning rate 0.2, varied the batch sizes from 512 to 2048, and used a standard pre-trained Wide Residual Network (WRN-28-10) model to generate 1M CIFAR-10/CIFAR-100 images. Moreover, to address the scarcity of annotated medical images, specifically blood cell images, authors introduced a diffusionbased Wasserstein GAN model for data augmentation. Their goal was to generate realistic synthetic blood cell images to improve the performance of machine learning models trained on these datasets. They trained their model on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0002 and batch size 4 and evaluated the quality of generated images via visual assessment and standard GAN metrics. Adversarial Diffusion Models are mostly designed to deal with image data and support various coding techniques, including Batch Normalization, Denoising Diffusion for generating synthetic images, Anatomical Masks to guide the reverse diffusion process to maintain anatomical information, and Weight Averaging to stabilize training." .


