###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ARDM
:ARDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :DiffusionModels ;
      :HasModality :GraphData ,
                   :ImageData ,
                   :TextData ,
                   :TimeSeriesData ;
      :UsesTechnique :DynamicProgramming ,
                     :LangevinSampling ,
                     :VariationalBounds ;
      rdfs:comment "Autoregressive Diffusion Models (ARDMs) combine the principles of Autoregressive Models (ARMs) and discrete diffusion models to generate data in any order while maintaining high efficiency and accuracy. These models do not require the causal masking typical of standard ARMs and leverage an efficient training objective similar to modern probabilistic diffusion models. ARDMs are advantageous for tasks like lossless compression and can be adapted for parallel generation, making them suitable for high-dimensional data modeling, including images, text, and audio. Traditional models often provide univariate forecasts and struggle with scaling to multiple correlated series. Intending to develop a method that accurately models the joint distribution of multiple time series, capturing their dependencies to improve forecast accuracy and uncertainty quantification, authors developed the TimeGrad model, an autoregressive model using diffusion probabilistic models, that estimate the gradient of the data distribution and employing a Markov chain with Langevin sampling to convert white noise into samples from the target distribution. The model learns the gradient by optimizing a variational bound on the data likelihood. They employed an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and a batch size of 64. They trained their model for six real-world datasets (Exchange, Solar, Electricity, Traffic, Taxi, Wikipedia) to evaluate the model’s performance. Autoregressive diffusion models support various data types, including image, text, graph, and time series, as well as can employ several coding techniques to enhance the model performance, such as Dynamic programming for parallelizing the generation and inference processes in ARDMs, Absorbing diffusion to facilitate efficient data modeling in discrete spaces, Langevin sampling for converting white noise into samples of interest through a Markov chain, and Variational bounds and Score matching to optimize the likelihood and gradients in ARDMs, particularly for probabilistic forecasting and denoising tasks." .


