###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/AlgorithmicBias
:AlgorithmicBias rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                 :HasConsequence :UnfairTreatmentRecommendations ;
                 :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                 :HasMitigation :BiasAuditsConduct ,
                                :BiasDetectionFrameworks ,
                                :CarefulImputationMethodsSelection ;
                 :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#BiasedTrainingData;FlawedDesign> ;
                 :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                 :RegulatedBy :AequitasFramework ,
                              :FairlearnFramework ,
                              :IBMAIFairness360Framework ;
                 :HasDetectabilityScore 8 ;
                 :HasLikelihoodScore 9 ;
                 :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                 rdfs:comment """Algorithmic bias occurs when data processing models produce skewed outcomes due to unbalanced training data, flawed model design, or cultural biases embedded in the data. Such biases can lead to unfair treatment recommendations, particularly affecting underrepresented groups. Several studies indicate that causes include biased training data, flawed model design, and inappropriate imputation techniques. Consequences include disparities in healthcare outcomes and loss of trust in data-driven systems. Mitigation techniques involve conducting bias audits, ensuring fairness in model design, and carefully selecting imputation methods to avoid reinforcing biases. Bias detection frameworks such as Fairlearn, Aequitas, or IBM AI Fairness 360 should be integrated to evaluate fairness metrics during development and deployment phases continuously.

Algorithmic bias can significantly impact health equity, leading to discriminatory outcomes. For example, predictive models in healthcare can disadvantage certain racial or socio-economic groups by skewing diagnoses or treatment recommendations based on biased data. As the Framingham Heart Study highlighted by Katherine J. Igoe, the cardiovascular risk score performed very well for Caucasian but not African American patients, leading to inequitable care. The potential for harm is high when biased models are integrated into clinical decision-making processes. The likelihood of bias is high, particularly when algorithms are trained on data that underrepresents vulnerable populations. Celi, Leo Anthony, et al. reported repeatedly feeding models with relatively homogeneous data can severely limit the generalisability of results and yield biased AIbased decisions. Moreover, many healthcare AI systems are built on historical data, which may carry embedded biases that are difficult to mitigate during the development process. Detecting bias can be difficult, as biases are often subtle and embedded within both the data and model design. Without transparency, diverse data sources, and continuous evaluation, the ability to detect bias remains low.""" .


