###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BART
:BART rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
      :HasModality :ImageData ,
                   :TextData ;
      :UsesTechnique :AutoRegressiveDecoding ,
                     :BidirectionalEncoding ,
                     :BytePairEncoding ,
                     :LayerNormalisation ,
                     :PositionalEncoding ;
      rdfs:comment "Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformer (BART) enhances coherent text/data generation by capturing context and improving semantic consistency through its unique architecture. It leverages bidirectional and auto-regressive transformers to achieve this, making it particularly effective for various natural language processing tasks. BART’s architecture combines the strengths of bidirectional and auto-regressive transformers. The encoder processes the entire input sequence bidirectionally, capturing context from both directions, while the decoder generates data auto-regressively, predicting each token based on the previous ones. This hybrid approach allows BART to excel in generating coherent and contextually relevant data. It typically employs a learning rate between 0.00003 and 0.0003 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence, authors proposed two pretraining objectives, inter-sentence similarity prediction, and sentence order discrimination, to learn the sentence level and discourse-level representations. They trained their model for the vocabulary dataset, which consisted of 50,625 tokens with Byte-Pair Encoding, at a learning rate of 0.00003 for both post-training and fine-tuning with Adam optimizer. They employed 6 hidden layers with 12 attention heads for the encoder and decoder, and the experiment demonstrated that the proposed model could generate more coherent texts with related context and proper sentence orders. Authors introduced a novel image inpainting framework that incorporates BART for this purpose. Their approach leverages transformers to learn autoregressive distributions, facilitating the diverse generation of missing content. They trained structure and texture generators separately from scratch using random irregular masks, employing the ADAM with Weight decay (AdamW) optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0003 for the structure generator and the Adam optimizer with learning rates of 0.0001 and 0.0004 for the generator and discriminator of the texture generator, respectively. BART can handle various text and image data types and is ideal for generating and transforming plain text, making it useful for summarization, translation, and text completion tasks. This model is effective for generating responses in dialogue systems, chatbots, and conversational agents, and it can be adapted to work with structured data. It employs several techniques, including bidirectional encoding, auto-regressive decoding, positional encoding, and layer normalization, to enhance performance" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BEGAN
:BEGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :FeatureMatching ,
                      :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ,
                      :NoiseInjection ;
       rdfs:comment """Boundary Equilibrium GANs (BEGANs) introduce a unique equilibrium concept between the generator and discriminator, enhancing both training stability and image quality. The detailed documentation of BEGAN’s architecture, training processes, and evaluation metrics ensures transparency and reproducibility. It typically uses learning rates starting from 0.0001, such as the authors in trained their model with Adam optimizer with initial learning rate 0.0001, the authors in employed learning rate 0.0002 using the same optimizer, and the authors in employed learning rate 0.001 for CelebA dataset and 0.0005 for Large-scale Scene Understanding (LSUN) bedroom dataset, for stabilizing the training process and ensuring convergence. They used different batch sizes, such as 1 and 16, to balance computational efficiency and model performance. Moreover, various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, can also be used to minimize the loss functions of BEGANs effectively. BEGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time
series data.""" .


