###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BiasInDataCollection
:BiasInDataCollection rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               :DataEntryAndCollection ,
                               :PatientReportedData ;
                      :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InappropriateTreatments;HealthcareDisparities> ;
                      :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                      :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
                      :HasMitigation :ErrorCheckingMechanisms ,
                                     :ThoroughDocumentation ;
                      :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Underreporting;SelectiveReporting> ;
                      :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                      :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
                      :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                      :HasSeverityScore 8 ;
                      rdfs:comment """Systematic biases during data collection, such as underreporting or selective reporting, can skew the data, leading to inappropriate generalizations and treatment decisions. Studies highlight how biases, whether intentional or unintentional, can distort data sets, potentially leading to disparities in healthcare outcomes and ineffective treatment plans.

Biased data can perpetuate healthcare inequalities and lead to misinformed decisions that affect patient care, such as incorrect treatment based on flawed algorithms. It has high severity, as addressed by Carrie Arnold in the Hopkins Bloomberg Public Health Magazine, through a discussion on biased data leading to systemic issues in healthcare, such as incorrect clinical decisions based on flawed data interpretations or biased algorithms. Al-Sahab et al. explored the context of inherent biases in data collection processes within EHR systems and the challenges they pose in producing reliable real-world evidence. The likelihood can be medium to high, given that bias can inadvertently be introduced through various stages of data handling, from collection to analysis. Detectability might be low, as identifying bias requires sophisticated analytical techniques and awareness of potential biases, which may not be apparent during routine data collection and processing.""" .


