###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BigBird
:BigBird rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
         :HasModality :ImageData ,
                      :TextData ,
                      :TimeSeriesData ;
         :UsesTechnique :ClassConditionalBatchNormalisation ,
                        :PositionalEncoding ,
                        :SelfAttentionMechanisms ,
                        :Tokenisation ;
         rdfs:comment """BigBird enhances traditional transformer models with a sparse attention mechanism, improving the handling of long data sequences while maintaining computational efficiency. This makes BigBird particularly effective for tasks involving lengthy documents, such as summarization, question answering, and language modeling. It retains the core components of transformer models, like self-attention and feed-forward layers, but introduces sparse attention patterns to reduce the quadratic complexity of the original self-attention mechanism. This
enables BigBird to process longer sequences that standard transformers cannot handle. By using block and random attention patterns, BigBird reduces computational load while still capturing long-range dependencies. It typically sets the learning rate between 0.00001 and 0.0001 for stabilizing the training process and ensuring model convergence, as well as supports larger batch sizes, often up to 2048, leveraging its efficient attention mechanism to handle large-scale data more effectively. Authors performed and shared BigBird experimental details for several NLP tasks, such as masked language model by setting the learning rate 0.0001for Adam optimizer and a batch size 256, HotpotQA, NaturalQ, TrivialQA, and WikiHop for Question Answering by adjusting the learning rates 0.00003, 0.00001, 0.00005, and 0.00001 for Adam and Layer-wise Adaptive Moments optimizer for Batch training (LAMB) optimizers and batch sizes 32 and 64. They also performed classification, summarization, and computational biology tasks with different learning rates and batch sizes by employing the Adam optimizer. BigBird is versatile, handling long text sequences for tasks like document summarization, translation, and question answering. It also adapts to image processing tasks like captioning and segmentation, benefiting from understanding long-range dependencies. Additionally, it effectively analyzes long time-series data, such as financial market predictions and patient health monitoring. BigBird employs several advanced techniques, such as Tokenization, Positional Encoding, Class-Conditional Batch Normalization, and Self-Attention Mechanisms, to enhance model performance and handle diverse data types.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BigGAN
:BigGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :GANs ;
        :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                           :TextData ;
        :UsesTechnique :AttentionSelfAttentionMechanism ,
                       :BatchNormalisation ,
                       :FeatureMatching ,
                       :NoiseInjection ;
        rdfs:comment "BigGAN employs an enlarged GAN architecture with attention mechanisms to generate high-resolution, realistic synthetic images, focusing intensively on essential data features to enhance both quality and contextual accuracy. BigGAN strongly emphasizes transparency and reproducibility, providing comprehensive documentation of its architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics. It typically uses finely-tuned learning rates, often in the range of 0.0001 to 0.0003, to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence, as well large batch sizes, often up to 2048, to improve gradient estimation and model performance, though smaller sizes like 64 or 128 may also be used depending on computational resources. The Adam optimizer is primarily used in BigGAN due to its adaptive learning rate capabilities and effectiveness in handling  complex loss functions. However, various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, AdaBelief, and SGD, can also be used to minimize the loss functions of BigGAN effectively, as well it supports image and text data." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/BivariateCorrelationMetrics
:BivariateCorrelationMetrics rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                      :CorrelationStructure ;
                             rdfs:comment "Bivariate Correlation Metrics: To check whether bivariate correlations are preserved in synthetic data, different correlation metrics can be used, such as Pearson’s r, Spearman’s ρ, Kendall’s τ , and ϕ coefficient. Results can be provided in tables or graphically in correlation plots." .


