###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CAEs
:CAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Contractive Autoencoders (CAEs) are designed to learn robust and invariant representations by imposing a penalty on the derivatives of the hidden representations with respect to the input. This regularization encourages the autoencoder to map similar inputs to closely clustered points in the latent space, resulting in a more compact and stable latent representation. CAEs are particularly effective in denoising and feature extraction tasks, providing enhanced stability and generalizability. It regularizes the latent space by penalizing the magnitude of the Jacobian and ensures that the learned representations are smooth and invariant to small input perturbations. CAEs involve precise tuning of hyperparameters to achieve optimal performance, authors introduced deep embedding clustering framework based on contractive autoencoder by employing SGD optimizer with learning rate 0.1 on 20NEWSGROUPS dataset to learn document representations, as well as employed learning rate 0.01 for other text and image datasets, such as MNIST, Fashion, USPS, Reuters-10K, and Reuters." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CAPALog
:CAPALog rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :RootCauseAnalysisEvidence .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CDAEs
:CDAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
       rdfs:comment "Cross-domain Autoencoders (CDAEs) specialize in transferring knowledge between domains by learning a shared representation that captures the underlying structure of source and target data. This capability makes them particularly useful for tasks such as sentiment analysis and image translation between datasets. The structure typically includes two primary components: the encoder, which compresses data from both domains into a shared latent space, and the decoder, which reconstructs the data back into their respective domains. Effective training of cross-domain autoencoders usually involves learning rates between 0.0001 and 0.003 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence. Authors trained their model for cross-domain sentiment classification by employing SGD optimizer with a learning rate of 0.003 and batch size 40 by utilizing multi-source domain adaptation with joint learning, whereas the authors in Ref. [198] used cross-domain auto-encoder and decoder and employed Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0002 for imageto-image translation. Authors developed coupled generative adversarial autoencoders, used ResNet-50 as the model structure for the classifier, and trained the net on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001, which allows a pair of generators to learn the high-level differences between two domains by sharing only part of the high-level layers. To improve model performance and ensure effective domain transfer, Cross-domain Autoencoders employ several advanced techniques, such as Domain-Invariant Feature Extraction, Conditional Batch Normalization, Adversarial Training, and Latent Space Alignment." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CDM
:CDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :AudioData ,
                  :ImageData ,
                  :TextData ,
                  :TimeSeriesData ;
     :UsesTechnique :ConditionalDiffusion ,
                    :DenoisingDiffusion ,
                    :ProbabilisticImputation ,
                    :ScoreMatchingWithLangevinDynamics-SMLD ;
     rdfs:comment "Conditional Diffusion Models (CDMs) are generative models that build on traditional diffusion models by integrating conditional information to guide the data generation process. These models operate through a forward diffusion process, progressively adding noise to the data, followed by a reverse diffusion process that denoises the data to reconstruct high-quality samples. CDMs are designed for tasks requiring conditional generation, such as super-resolution, image inpainting, MRI reconstruction, time series imputation, and high-quality speech synthesis. Their ability to incorporate conditional information enables them to generate contextually relevant outputs, improving performance across various applications. Traditional DDPMs have shown promising results in generative tasks but are hindered by slow iterative sampling processes, making them impractical for real-time applications. Authors introduced FastDiff, a fast conditional diffusion model, by employing time-aware location-variable convolutions to model long-term dependencies and a noise schedule predictor to reduce sampling steps without compromising quality. The goal was to enable high-fidelity speech synthesis significantly faster than in real-time on standard GPUs. They trained their model on the Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0002 and a batch size 16. They performed experiments on datasets involving LJSpeech (for standard evaluation) and Voice Cloning ToolKit (VCTK) (for testing generalization to unseen speakers). Traditional text recognition systems often struggle with the diversity and complexity of real-world text images due to the difficulty in collecting and annotating sufficient real data. The authors in Ref. [339] developed Conditional Text Image Generation with Diffusion Models (CTIG-DM) to address the challenge of generating high-quality and diverse text images using diffusion models. This model integrates three conditions (image, text, and style) to control generated text images’ attributes, contents, and styles. They trained their proposed model on the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and set batch size 64 for training the text recognizer and 256 for training the diffusion models. Conditional Diffusion Models support various data types, including image, text, Time Series, and Audio data. These models incorporate several advanced techniques and coding methodologies, such as Score Matching with Langevin Dynamics (SMLD) to estimate data distribution and apply Langevin dynamics for sampling, Denoising Diffusion for adding Gaussian noise to data and then denoises it in a discrete manner to generate high-quality samples, Conditional Diffusion Process to guide the reverse diffusion process, and Probabilistic Imputation for improving imputation quality by leveraging temporal dependencies." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CGAN
:CGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :GANs ;
      :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                         :TextData ,
                         :TimeSeriesData ;
      :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                     :FeatureMatching ,
                     :NoiseInjection ;
      rdfs:comment "Conditional GANs (CGANs) enhance traditional GANs by conditioning both the generator and discriminator with additional labels, which improves the relevance and specificity of the generated images. This approach allows CGANs to produce high-quality, category-specific synthetic data, making them highly useful for various applications, including healthcare, where specific and detailed synthetic datasets are often required. CGANs are designed to focus on detailed documentation of their architecture and training procedures, ensuring transparency and reproducibility. The model architecture involves conditioning the generator and discriminator on auxiliary information, such as class labels, which guides the generation process and improves the accuracy of the discriminator. It typically employs learning rates between 0.00001 and 0.01 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence, such as the authors iemployed Adam optimizer and learning rates of 0.0001, 0.001, and 0.01 to train their model with batch size 100, and other authors employed learning rate 0.00001 with a batch size 32 to train Modified National Institute of Standards and Technology (MNIST) dataset. Moreover, various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, can also be used to minimize the loss functions of CGAN effectively, as well as it supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data." .


