###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClassifierAUCROC
:ClassifierAUCROC rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :Distinguishability ,
                           :UtilityMetrics ;
                  rdfs:comment """Instead of accuracy, AUCROC is another metric for binary classification. The metric indicates the ability of a classifier to discriminate between positively and negatively labeled samples and is thus a more natural metric for the aforementioned task of discriminating synthetic from real data. Because of this, it is also more interpretable in the case of imbalanced data. A perfect score, meaning the classifier is unable to discriminate synthetic from real samples, equates to 0.5.

For Utility: for classification tasks""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClassifierAccuracy
:ClassifierAccuracy rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             :Distinguishability ;
                    rdfs:comment """Classification of synthetic versus real data can serve as a proxy for measuring
similarity in their underlying distribution. Real and synthetic data receive opposite binary labels, after which a classifier is trained and scored on independent train and test sets. Effectively, the classifier univariately compresses the data into a classification score, where differences in the original feature space will result in differences in this score. When synthetic and real samples receive similar classification scores, this indicates that the original data is also similar. Classification provides an interpretable and flexible method to assess synthetic data similarity to real data. The choice of classifier can accommodate any data type, and multiple levels of discriminability (e.g., linear and non-linear) can be investigated by using different classifiers. Herein also lies the challenge; since classifiers can be sensitive to underlying assumptions, initialization, and hyperparameters, a wide suite of classifiers should be tested to gain a proper understanding of synthetic to real similarity. Accuracy is an interpretable metric to assess performance in this classification task. It shows the proportion of correctly classified samples for a given threshold, usually 0.5. A perfect score, meaning the classifier is unable to discriminate synthetic from real samples, equates to 0.5 (for a balanced dataset). Since this metric is insensitive to class imbalance, it can potentially provide skewed interpretations of results when synthetic and real datasets are of different sizes.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClassifierFreeGuidance
:ClassifierFreeGuidance rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClassifierpMSE
:ClassifierpMSE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         :Distinguishability ;
                rdfs:comment "Another metric that indicates the performance of a classifier discriminating synthetic from real data is propensity Mean Squared Error (pMSE). It indicates the mean squared error between classification scores and the proportion of synthetic data in the total dataset (real and synthetic merged). Thus, it indicates the classifier’s ability to outperform predictions corresponding to the proportionality of the labels. Low scores (close to 0) indicate the classifier is unable to accurately discriminate synthetic from real samples" .


