###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClusterAnalysisMeasure
:ClusterAnalysisMeasure rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :Distinguishability ;
                        rdfs:comment "Next to classifiers, clustering algorithms can be utilized to investigate real and synthetic data distinguishability. The cluster analysis measure performs clustering on a merged dataset of synthetic and real samples and indicates whether clusters contain real and synthetic samples in the same proportion as the original dataset. When this is not the case, this potentially indicates that real and synthetic data are dissimilar. The measure computes the mean squared error between the proportion of synthetic samples in a cluster and the overall proportion of synthetic samples in the original dataset across clusters" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClusteringBasedPrototypeModeling
:ClusteringBasedPrototypeModeling rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                           :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ClusteringModels
:ClusteringModels rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :BasicMLMethods ;
                  rdfs:comment """Clustering methods are similar to the SMOTE algorithm in that they cluster the data into classes and, from there, generate new observations. However, a key difference with SMOTE is that every class is clustered independently/separately, new observations are randomly drawn from the cluster, and the user can typically decide on the clustering algorithm. An example of a clustering model for data generation
is the Cluster-Based Oversampling algorithm. Clustering models face the same issues as SMOTE in that they are oblivious to the underlying distribution of the data.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CoGAN
:CoGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ;
       rdfs:comment """Coupled GAN (CoGAN) enhances traditional GAN architectures by employing a pair of coupled networks to learn joint distributions of multi-domain images, such as Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) and Computerised Tomography (CT) scans, which is highly beneficial for synthetic healthcare dataset generation. This dual-network architecture allows CoGAN to generate aligned image sets from different domains, improving the quality and consistency of synthetic data across varied medical imaging modalities. CoGAN’s methodology is well-documented, detailing its dual-network architecture and training procedures. Each network in the pair is responsible for generating images in a specific domain, and the networks share weights in the lower layers to ensure that the generated images are aligned across domains. It typically employs learning rates around 0.0002 to stabilize the training process and ensure effective convergence of both networks, such as some authors employed Adam optimizer and learning rates of 0.0001, 0.0002, and 0.0004 to train their models with batch size 128 to balance the computational efficiency and model performance. Moreover, various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, can also be used to minimize the loss functions of CoGAN effectively, as well as support various data types, including
image, text, and time series data.""" .


