###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CyberAttacks
:CyberAttacks rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       :EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazards ;
              :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DataBreaches;FinancialLosses> ;
              :HasDetectability :LowToMediumDetectability ;
              :HasLikelihood :HighLikelihood ;
              :HasMitigation :AdvancedSecurityMeasures ,
                             :ContinuousVulnerabilityIdentificationAndRemediation ,
                             :EmployeesTrainingOnPhishingRisks ,
                             :IncidentResponseAndCoordinatedVulnerabilityDisclosure ,
                             :IncidentResponsePlans ,
                             :PostDeploymentMonitoring ,
                             :RegularSoftwareUpdates ,
                             :SBOMCreationAndMaintenance ,
                             :SecureSDLCAndCodeReview ,
                             :SecurityLoggingAndAnomalyDetection ,
                             :ThreatModelling ;
              :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#WeakCybersecurity;UnPatchedSoftware> ;
              :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
              :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
              :HasLikelihoodScore 9 ;
              :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
              rdfs:comment """Cyber-attacks, including malware, ransomware, and denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, pose significant threats to healthcare data security. These attacks are often launched by external actors aiming to exploit system vulnerabilities, disrupting operations, compromising patient data, and causing financial losses. Causes include weak cybersecurity measures, unpatched software, and phishing vulnerabilities. The consequences involve data breaches, service outages, and costly recovery efforts. Mitigation strategies focus on deploying advanced security measures, ensuring regular software updates, training employees on phishing risks, and establishing incident response plans to quickly address and contain attacks. Concretely, there is a need to apply threat modelling (e.g., STRIDE/attack trees), secure SDLC and code review, SBOM creation and maintenance, continuous vulnerability identification and remediation (patching/update mechanisms), security logging and anomaly detection, incident response and coordinated vulnerability disclosure, and postdeployment monitoring. This ensures that ransomware, DoS, and supply-chain threats are addressed through controls that meet health-sector cybersecurity expectations across pre-market and post-market phases.

Cyber-attacks have had far-reaching impacts on healthcare. Ransomware attacks, for example, have delayed medical procedures and compromised patient data, potentially putting patients’ lives at risk, especially in critical departments, which mark the severity high. The WHO highlighted these dangers during the COVID-19
pandemic when healthcare systems were targeted. The likelihood of cyber-attacks on healthcare systems has increased in recent years as these systems become more digitally dependent. With inadequate security measures in place, the healthcare sector has become a prime target for cyber-criminals. While advanced intrusion detection systems exist, sophisticated attacks often evade immediate detection, leaving healthcare organizations vulnerable until significant damage occurs, as highlighted in the WHO report.""" .


