:CycleConsistencyLoss rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CycleGAN
:CycleGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :GANs ;
          :GeneratesModality :ImageData ;
          :UsesTechnique :CycleConsistencyLoss ,
                         :LabelSmoothing ;
          rdfs:comment "CycleGAN is an unsupervised image-to-image translation model that enables transformations between different data domains without needing paired samples. This makes it particularly useful for photo enhancement and medical image conversion tasks. The architecture of CycleGAN includes two sets of generators and discriminators that work in the cycle to learn the mappings between two domains, ensuring that transformations are consistent and realistic. CycleGAN employs a range of hyperparameters to optimize its performance, and it supports image and voice data. It typically employs a learning rate of 0.0002 for both the generator and the discriminator for maintaining training stability and convergence and often uses batch sizes ranging from 1 to 32 for optimizing computational efficiency and model performance. Authors employed a learning rate of 0.002 with a batch size of 5 to assess the performance of CycleGAN using RMSprop, ADAM, and SGD optimizers. Moreover, the authors in improved the voice conversion by training the CycleGAN at a learning rate of 0.0002 with a batch size 1, and authors in improved the Mitosis detection by training the CycleGAN at a learning rate of 0.00002 with a batch size 32." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DALL-E
:DALL-E rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
        :HasModality :ImageData ,
                     :TextData ;
        :UsesTechnique :CrossAttentionMechanisms ,
                       :ImageEncoding ,
                       :Tokenisation ;
        rdfs:comment "DALL·E, DALL·E 2, and DALL·E 3, developed by OpenAI, are advanced models that generate digital images from text descriptions using a GPT-based architecture. These models employ a transformer-based design to combine textual and visual information effectively. Key components include GPT architecture, Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training embeddings, and spatial relation heads, emphasizing architecture design, training processes, and evaluation metrics for high-quality image generation. Authors discussed how to set up collaborative training and trained text-to-image transformer by employing an 8-bit LAMB optimizer with a peak learning rate of 0.0025 and showed that the resulting model generated images with reasonable quality on a number of prompts. DALL·E utilizes diverse coding techniques, including tokenization, image encoding, and cross-attention mechanisms, to optimize its performance. Textual descriptions undergo subword tokenization to enable the handling of extensive vocabulary and nuanced descriptions, while visual features are processed using CNNs or vision transformers to convert images into a compatible format. DALL·E and its variants are versatile in managing various image and text data types. Image data is predominant, enabling tasks such as generating realistic images aligned with textual descriptions that are applicable in fields like artistic concept creation, interface design, and educational illustrations. DALL·E models interpret intricate textual inputs well-suited for tasks requiring precise and detailed image generation from textual prompts." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DCGAN
:DCGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ;
       rdfs:comment """Deep Convolutional GAN (DCGAN) enhances traditional GAN architectures by incorporating convolutional layers, significantly improving the structural integrity and quality of the synthetic image. This refinement makes DCGAN particularly suitable for generating detailed healthcare datasets. DCGAN is designed strongly emphasizing transparency and reproducibility, offering comprehensive documentation of its convolutional architecture and training procedures. It typically employs a learning rate between 0.0001 and 0.1 for stabilizing the training process and ensuring smooth convergence, such some authors trained the DCGAN by employing the Adam optimizer with the learning rate 0.0001 and a batch size 128 for fingerprint sample synthesis, and the authors in employed the SGD optimizer to improve the robustness and training of DCGAN for Canadian Institute for Advanced Research, 10 classes (CIFAR10) dataset by setting the initial learning rate at 0.1. Moreover, various
optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, can also be used to minimize the loss functions of DCGAN effectively, as well as support image and time series data.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DDM
:DDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :AudioData ,
                  :ImageData ,
                  :TextData ,
                  :TimeSeriesData ;
     :UsesTechnique :LangevinDynamics ,
                    :MarkovChain ,
                    :MaskingAndLogitAdjustment ,
                    :MultinomialNoiseDistribution ,
                    :ScoreBasedLearning ,
                    :TransitionProbabilityMatrix ;
     rdfs:comment "Discrete Diffusion Models (DDM) adapt the traditional diffusion framework to handle discrete data, using a transition probability matrix to manage data corruption and restoration through a Markov process. These models, such as those used in text-to-sound generation and electronic health records, address the challenge of working with discrete variables by employing techniques like score-based learning and continuous-time Markov chains to improve estimation and sampling. Their purposes range from generating high-quality audio from text to enhancing synthetic EHR data generation and creating structured layouts for various applications. These models provide robust generative capabilities for discrete data, supporting tasks that require conditional and high-quality output generation. Traditional autoregressive token-decoders in sound generation suffer from unidirectional bias and error accumulation, leading to suboptimal performance and slower speeds. To overcome these issues, authors proposed Diffsound, a non-autoregressive token-decoder based on a discrete diffusion model, enhancing efficiency and quality in text-to-sound generation. Their developed framework includes a text encoder, a Vector Quantized Variational Autoencoder (VQ-VAE), a token decoder, and a vocoder. The text encoder extracts features, which the VQ-VAE processes to generate mel-spectrogram tokens. Diffsound predicts and iteratively refines these tokens simultaneously, using a discrete diffusion model to address AR model limitations. The vocoder then converts the mel-spectrogram into a waveform. The model uses transition matrices for token manipulation and incorporates a mask-based text generation strategy and curriculum learning to enhance performance. The experimental setup, which involves training VQ-VAE on a batch size of 20 and a learning rate of 0.000001 with Adam optimizer and Autoregressive Token-Decoder on a batch size of 16 and learning rate of 0.000003 with AdamW optimizer, demonstrated significant improvements in both subjective and objective metrics. Authors addressed the challenge of extending score-based diffusion models to discrete spaces, where traditional models struggle due to the undefined nature of score functions for discrete variables. They introduced a continuous-time discrete diffusion model using a stochastic jump process and continuous-time Markov chains for effective data corruption and denoising. The methodology involves defining score functions for categorical variables and employing categorical ratio matching for learning. Experiments on synthetic data, CIFAR10, and monophonic music datasets demonstrated the model’s effectiveness. Key hyperparameters include using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-41e-41e-4 and batch sizes of 128 for synthetic and CIFAR10 data and 64 for music data. Architectural choices include energy-based models, masked models, and hollow transformers to efficiently handle the discrete diffusion process. Discrete Diffusion Models support various data types, including text, image, audio, and time series, as well as can adapt various coding techniques, such as Transition probability matrix and Markov processes for data corruption and reconstruction, Score-based learning and Markov chains to extend diffusion models to discrete variables, Multinomial noise distribution for discrete data, Langevin dynamics for conditional generation, and Masking and Logit adjustment to handle structured layout data and support conditional generation tasks." .


