###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataAnonymisation
:DataAnonymisation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                            :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataAnonymisationErrors
:DataAnonymisationErrors rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                         :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PrivacyBreaches;ReducedDataUtility> ;
                         :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                         :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                         :HasMitigation :AdvancedAnonymisationTechniques ,
                                        :DeIdentificationTechniques ,
                                        :StructuredValidation ;
                         :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#WeakTechniques;ExcessiveAnonymisation> ;
                         :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                         :HasDetectabilityScore 6 ;
                         :HasLikelihoodScore 8 ;
                         :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                         rdfs:comment """Data anonymization errors occur when personally identifiable information (PII) is either inadequately removed, thereby risking patient privacy, or overly anonymized, resulting in the loss of critical data for analysis. Causes include weak anonymization techniques and overzealous anonymization efforts, with consequences such as privacy breaches and reduced data utility. Mitigation strategies involve using advanced anonymization techniques such as differential privacy and ensuring a balance between privacy and data utility. Methods such as k-anonymity, l-diversity, t-closeness, and differential privacy provide mathematically grounded guarantees of de-identification. Automated risk assessment tools, such as ARX or sdcMicro, can support structured validation, ensuring consistent and repeatable anonymization processes.

Anonymization errors can lead to privacy breaches, particularly in health data that contains sensitive personal information. This breach can expose individuals to risks, including identity theft and potential discrimination. Olatunji, Iyiola E., et al. mentioned in their study that anonymization achieved with generalization-only approaches inevitably distorts the records. Therefore, over-generalization negatively influences the analysis of the anonymized dataset, which emphasizes the severity of data anonymization errors is high. Moreover, Abouelmehdi, K., et al. highlighted the privacy and security issues in big data, mentioning that attackers can use data mining methods to uncover sensitive data and release it to the public, which could have severe consequences. While robust anonymization techniques, such as k-anonymity and differential privacy, have been developed, their implementation can still be complex, especially in large datasets. Inaccurate
anonymization can occur when these techniques are not correctly applied, which emphasizes the likelihood of anonymization errors is medium. Vovk, O., et al. highlighted that anonymization methods, while helpful, do not entirely prevent re-identification attacks, especially when combined with external datasets. Detecting anonymization errors is challenging because the data appears to be anonymized; however, re-identification attacks, if successful, can expose vulnerabilities only after a breach occurs, resulting in low detectability.""" .


