###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMisinterpretation
:DataMisinterpretation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                       :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ClinicalMisjudgement;ReducedCareQuality> ;
                       :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                       :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                       :HasMitigation :ClearActionableInsightsProvision ,
                                      :CommunicatoinBetweenDataExpertsAndClinicians ,
                                      :TrainingOnInterpretingDataAnalysisResults ;
                       :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ComplexModels;PoorCommunication> ;
                       :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                       :HasDetectabilityScore 4 ;
                       :HasLikelihoodScore 9 ;
                       :HasSeverityScore 7 ;
                       rdfs:comment """Data misinterpretation happens when the outputs of data analysis are misunderstood or misapplied by healthcare providers, often due to the complexity of the models or inadequate communication of results. Literature indicates several causes, including the complexity of models, ineffective communication, and overconfidence in technology, with consequences that include clinical misjudgment and reduced quality of care. Mitigation strategies involve providing clear, actionable insights, improving communication between data experts and clinicians, and offering training on interpreting data analysis results.

Data misinterpretation is often ranked with a high severity due to the critical nature of medical decisions that rely on accurate data interpretation. Lesley Clack mentioned the facts of predictive analytics in her report ‘predictive analytics used by health systems to create platforms for predicting and preventing the common and costly diagnoses, provide enhanced accuracy in predicting length of stay, and help providers personalize treatments’, which highlights the severity and potential consequences of data misinterpretation in predictive analytics. The likelihood of occurrence is generally moderate, depending on the complexity of the data and the
expertise of the healthcare professionals involved. Detectability tends to be medium because errors can be subtle and not immediately obvious without thorough validation procedures. Shannon H. Houser highlighted the detectability of data misinterpretation through visualization tools as follows: Effective data visualization is crucial for health professionals as it enables them to convey insights efficiently. They use visualizations to simplify complex health information, making it easier for stakeholders to understand. With effective visualizations, health professionals can identify patterns and trends in healthcare data, leading to informed decisions and improved patient outcomes.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMismatch
:DataMismatch rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       :SanityChecks ;
              rdfs:comment """To check whether feature data types match between synthetic and real data, data
mismatch provides the proportion of matching data types over columns between synthetic and real data""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataOverload
:DataOverload rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       :DataTransmissionAndSharing ;
              :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemCrashes;DataLoss> ;
              :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
              :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
              :HasMitigation :CompressionTechniques ,
                             :DataPrioritisation ,
                             :ScalableCloudBasedSolutions ;
              :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InadequateProtocols;SystemConflicts> ,
                                 :ExcessiveDataTransmission ;
              :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
              :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
              :HasLikelihoodScore 7 ;
              :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
              rdfs:comment """Excessive data volumes can overwhelm systems, causing performance degradation or data loss. Literature suggests that data overload is a growing concern in high-data environments, such as intensive care units. Overload is typically caused by the transmission of excessive data, resulting in system crashes or slowdowns. The consequences include delayed or lost data, which can impact care quality. Solutions include data prioritization, compression techniques, and scalable cloud-based solutions to manage large data volumes efficiently.

Data overload can impede real-time decision-making and lead to information fatigue for healthcare providers. When too much data flows into systems without adequate filtering, it becomes challenging to extract relevant insights quickly, which can delay critical interventions in patient care. Merhej, Joseph, et al. highlighted the risks associated with data overload. When too much data flows into systems without adequate filtering, it becomes challenging to extract relevant insights quickly, which can delay critical interventions. This demonstrates how data overload can severely impact timely decision-making in healthcare settings The increased reliance on IoT devices and the constant generation of data across healthcare settings make data overload a frequent issue, which increases the likelihood of a medium to high level. Large-scale data sharing, particularly during crises such as the COVID-19 pandemic, has underscored the risks associated with managing vast amounts of data. De Kok, Jip WTM, et al. pointed out the challenges of managing vast data volumes securely and efficiently during exchanges. The detectability can be medium, as while monitoring systems can detect data volume spikes, they may not effectively distinguish between essential and non-essential data, making it challenging to filter information in real-time without specific algorithms tailored for data relevance.""" .


