###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataRoutingAndSwitchingErrors
:DataRoutingAndSwitchingErrors rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :DataTransmissionAndSharing ;
                               :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PrivacyBreaches;TreatmentDelays> ;
                               :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                               :HasLikelihood :RareToMediumLikelihood ;
                               :HasMitigation :AccurateConfigurationOfRoutingTables ,
                                              :ErrorCheckingProtocols ,
                                              :ReduntantNetworkPathsEmployment ;
                               :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Misconfigurations;NetworkCongestion> ;
                               :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                               :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                               :HasLikelihoodScore 4 ;
                               :HasSeverityScore 8 ;
                               rdfs:comment """Routing and switching errors occur when data is incorrectly directed or lost during transmission, resulting in delays or data breaches. Literature points out that these errors can disrupt patient care and compromise data security. Misrouted data is often caused by misconfigurations or network congestion, resulting in privacy breaches and treatment delays. Strategies include the accurate configuration of routing tables, the use of error-checking protocols, and the employment of redundant network paths to ensure data integrity.

Errors in data routing can lead to data loss or misdirection, impacting patient confidentiality and treatment with high severity. Concerning cybersecurity in hospitals, Argaw, Salem T., et al. highlighted the case of Hancock Regional Hospital, which experienced a ransomware attack by the malware SamSam. Following the attack, the hospital’s IT team shut down all network and desktop systems, which reveals the high severity of routing errors and their impact on hospital operations. Given that routing protocols are designed to be robust, the likelihood might be low to medium, as highlighted by Rahul Varshneya related to data security measures \"To be able to focus on its core business, a healthcare organisation must secure health information\" underscores the necessity of robust routing protocols to prevent errors that could compromise sensitive data. The detectability of these errors is often marked as medium, as while some errors can be detected quickly by network monitoring tools, others might only be discovered through the manifestation of data access or performance issues.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStewardshipAssigmentsRecordsEvidenceLog
:DataStewardshipAssigmentsRecordsEvidenceLog rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                      :GovernanceRolesEvidence .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSychronisationFailures
:DataSychronisationFailures rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                     :DataTransmissionAndSharing ;
                            :HasConsequence :OutdatedOrConflictingInformation ;
                            :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                            :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                            :HasMitigation :ResolutionMechanisms ;
                            :HasModality :SychronisationProtocols ;
                            :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InadequateProtocols;SystemConflicts> ;
                            :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                            :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                            :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                            :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                            rdfs:comment """Data synchronization failures occur when updates are not consistently reflected across systems, leading to discrepancies such as conflicting patient information. Literature indicates these failures can cause outdated or conflicting information, risking patient safety. Synchronization issues often result from inadequate protocols or system conflicts, leading to different data versions across platforms. Implementing robust synchronization protocols and conflict resolution mechanisms can ensure data consistency and accuracy across systems.

Failures in data synchronization can lead to mismatched patient records and inconsistencies across healthcare systems. This can lead to erroneous treatment decisions if one system reflects outdated or incomplete information, which can be particularly dangerous in emergencies and has high-severity implications. A recent report on healthcare data storage challenges by Acronis, \"Interoperability is key to handling health data, as it is shared between different parties as the patient receives care. Allowing the authorized access, exchange, and cooperative use of important patient information within a health care organization or transversely with regional or national parties directly impacts the efficiency, quality, and safety of the patient’s experience,\" explains the severity of synchronization issues on patient care. The synchronization processes are usually automated and monitored; the likelihood is often medium, but failures can still occur due to system errors or misconfigurations. From the discussion on healthcare data management challenges in the KMS healthcare report, such as establishing policies for data retention, archiving, and deletion based on regulatory requirements and the data’s
ongoing value to the organization, highlights the importance of synchronization in maintaining data integrity and the impacts of failures. These failures are often detectable through periodic checks and data audits, although real-time detection can be more challenging without advanced interoperability and monitoring tools.""" .


