###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransitClaim
:DataTransitClaim rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :SecurityAndPrivacy ;
                  :HasContext :EncryptedDataContext ;
                  :JustifiedBy :MinimizedExposureRisksJustification ;
                  :ProducesEvidence :EncryptionConfigurationsLog ,
                                    :KeyManagementPoliciesLog ,
                                    :TLSCertificatesLog ;
                  :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27002:2022Standard> ,
                               :NISTSP800-53Standard ;
                  rdfs:comment "Data in transit and at rest is protected" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionErrors
:DataTransmissionErrors rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :ClinicalDataHazards ,
                                 :DataTransmissionAndSharing ;
                        :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DelayedDecisions;ImpactedCare> ;
                        :HasDetectability :MediumToHighDetectability ;
                        :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
                        :HasMitigation :EncryptionAndDataIntegrity ,
                                       :ErrorDetectionProtocols ,
                                       :InteroperabilityRecommendations ,
                                       :RegulatoryAlignment ,
                                       :SecureSychronisation ,
                                       :SecurityControls ;
                        :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#NetworkIssues;HumanErrors> ;
                        :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                        :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27001:2022Standard> ,
                                     <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO27799:2016Standard> ,
                                     <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Regulation2017/745Standard> ,
                                     :CyberSecurityInMedicalDevicesStandard ,
                                     :DICOMStandard ,
                                     :HealthLevel7FHIRStandard ,
                                     :IEC81001-5-1Standard ,
                                     :IHEProfilesStandard ,
                                     :NISTAES-256Standard ;
                        :HasDetectabilityScore 4 ;
                        :HasLikelihoodScore 7 ;
                        :HasSeverityScore 8 ;
                        rdfs:comment """Transmission errors in healthcare data can include incomplete transfers, delays, or misrouted data, resulting in the loss of critical patient information and potential treatment errors. Literature indicates that these errors can arise from network issues, system failures, or human errors in routing data. Consequences of data transmission errors range from delayed patient care to incorrect treatment due to incomplete or late data [47, 48, 49]. Mitigation strategies include implementing error-detection protocols, such as checksums, and real-time monitoring to address transmission delays. Additionally, ensuring secure routing mechanisms is crucial to maintaining data integrity and ensuring timely delivery. Encryption and data-integrity measures follow NIST AES-256 for data at rest and TLS 1.3 for data in transit, supported by ISO/IEC 27001 information security management practices. Interoperability recommendations adopt HL7 FHIR, IHE profiles (such as XDS, XCA, ATNA) [50, 51, 52], and DICOM for imaging. At the same time, secure synchronisation and health-sector security controls are guided by ISO/IEC 27799 and IEC 81001-5-1. For regulatory alignment, we also need to consider the FDA Cybersecurity in Medical Devices guidance and the EU Medical Device Regulation (MDR 2017/745) to ensure that transmission safeguards support both technical and regulatory expectations.

The transmission errors can lead to incorrect data being recorded or used in patient care, which could have serious consequences for treatment outcomes. Anne Trafton reported in MIT News that healthcare data breaches had occurred due to transmission errors, affecting nearly 100 million people, which underscores the high severity and likelihood of such events in the healthcare sector. Moreover, Jacob Berezin recently discussed healthcare data security trends and challenges in the CYOP Healthcare Cybersecurity report. He mentioned various vulnerabilities, including the risk of cyberattacks like DDoS, which can compromise patient data security, illustrating the likelihood and detectability of data breaches, often medium to high, depending on the complex systems involved in healthcare data transmission, which can introduce errors, particularly where integration between different systems is poor.""" .


