###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DecisionTreeClassifier
:DecisionTreeClassifier rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :UtilityMetrics .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DecisionTrees
:DecisionTrees rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        :BasicMLMethods ;
               rdfs:comment "Decision trees are among the simplest, non-parametric machine learning models featuring humaninterpretable structures. Decision trees iteratively split the data into subsets according to cutoff values of features so that each observation belongs to one final subset. Various algorithms for finding cutoff values exist, for example, based on minimizing the variance of a feature with respect to another target feature or based on entropy and impurity-based criteria. Interpretation is considered an important aspect of synthetic data generation, particularly in domains like healthcare. Interpretability contributes to the trustworthiness of the model and provides insights for further improvement. It is also a key aspect of explainable AI and has a crucial role in clinical decision-making. We discuss three examples of decision trees in the clinical domain. The authors in apply Randomized Decision Trees (RDTs) to generate privacy-preserved and scalable synthetic data. They trained their model on synthetic and real datasets and showed similar feature rankings, which indicates a high resemblance between synthetic and real datasets. The RDT structure was found to inherit the differential privacy requirements. Another example employing decision trees investigated the impact of variable order in sequential decision trees for data generation. The authors use a sequential synthetic data generation approach by altering variables. The approach operates in multiple cycles where every cycle generates one feature. The synthetic data is generated in two steps. In the first, a tree is created based on the selected features. In the second step, a synthetic sample from this tree is generated. Every novel feature tree is dependent on the features generated previously. The authors show that the order of features affects the quality of the synthetically generated data. The authors in use sequential decision trees to mimic the Ontario case database consisting of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19) positive records and conclude that the generated synthetic data provides promising fidelity and data privacy suitable for research and development." .


