###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EBDM
:EBDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :DiffusionModels ;
      :HasModality :ImageData ,
                   :TextData ;
      :UsesTechnique :EnergyBasedParameterisation ,
                     :FisherDivergence ,
                     :GeometricClustering ,
                     :SymbolVectorCoupling ;
      rdfs:comment "Energy-Based Diffusion Models (EBDMs) are utilized across various domains to enhance generative and classification tasks. In image synthesis and classification, EBDMs leverage Fisher divergence to train models that simultaneously generate high-quality images and accurate classifications. For interpretable text modeling, combining latent diffusion models with EBMs improves the quality and structure of generated text. To address the challenges of MCMC sampling in high-dimensional spaces, diffusion-amortized MCMC methods are employed, enhancing convergence and sampling efficiency. Additionally, EBDMs facilitate the reuse and composition of pre-trained diffusion models, allowing for flexible and efficient generative processes across multiple data modalities. To address the challenge of generating high-quality sound effects from text descriptions to overcome the limitations of traditional autoregressive token-decoders, which suffer from unidirectional bias and prediction errors, the authors in ref. [346] proposed Diffsound, a non-autoregressive token-decoder based on a discrete diffusion model, to enhance efficiency and quality in text-to-sound generation. The framework includes a text encoder, VQ-VAE, token-decoder, and vocoder, working together to extract features, generate mel-spectrogram tokens, and convert them into waveforms. The model uses transition matrices for token manipulation and incorporates a mask-based text generation strategy and curriculum learning for improved performance. Experiments conducted on the AudioSet dataset using the Adam optimizer (learning rate: 0.0001, batch size: 32) demonstrate superior generation quality and speed, evaluated through metrics like Frechet Inception Distance (FID), KL-divergence, and audio caption loss. Energy-Based diffusion models mostly deal with text and images, as well as can adapt various coding techniques, such as Fisher divergence for training the EGC models, softmax for classification, variational learning in Latent Diffusion Energy-Based models, geometric clustering-based regularization and symbol-vector coupling for interpretable text modeling, and energy-based parameterization and compositional generation for flexible and efficient generative processes." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EBGAN
:EBGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :FeatureMatching ,
                      :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ;
       rdfs:comment "The Energy-Based Generative Adversarial Network (EBGAN) utilizes an energy-based model with a margin loss function in its discriminator, enhancing the generation of stable, high-quality synthetic images. This approach is particularly effective for healthcare datasets, offering robust and reliable synthetic data generation. EBGAN’s methodology is thoroughly documented, providing detailed descriptions of its architecture, training protocols, and evaluation metrics. The authors in [116] introduced EBGAN by training the probabilistic GAN to show more stable behavior than regular GANs during training. They employed learning rates 0.0005 and 0.001, trained the model with an Adam optimizer on a batch size of 100, and showed that a singlescale architecture can be trained to generate high-resolution images. Recently, a comparative analysis of the information-theoretic and energy-based GAN model was also performed, in which the authors employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and compared the performance by setting the batch size to 4 for Pix2Pix, 1 for CycleGAN, 64 for SAGAN, and 16 for StyleGAN2 with an initial learning rate of 0.0002. Moreover, SGD can also be used to minimize the loss functions effectively. EBGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data." .


