###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ELECTRA
:ELECTRA rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
         :HasModality :TextData ;
         :UsesTechnique :PositionalEncoding ,
                        :ReplacementTokenDetection ,
                        :TokenEmbedding ;
         rdfs:comment """ELECTRA revolutionizes pre-training in natural language processing by employing a unique approach where a discriminator model differentiates between real and synthetic tokens, thus leveraging more input data for faster and more efficient training. This method competes with or surpasses traditional models like BERT
in performance and training speed. ELECTRA’s training involves two primary components: Generator – A smaller network that generates corrupted text by replacing some original tokens with plausible alternatives, and Discriminator – A larger network that identifies which tokens in the input sequence were replaced by the generator. Authors introduced ELECTRA as pre-training text encoders as discriminators rather than generators, in which they trained a discriminative model that predicts whether each token in the corrupted input was replaced by a generator sample or not, instead of training a model that predicts the original identities of the corrupted tokens. They employed Adam optimizers with learning rates 0.0005, 0.0002, and 0.0002 and batch sizes 128, 256, and 2048 for small, base, and large models. They showed ELECTRA outperforms other SOTA models. The authors in Ref. [278] developed BioM-ELECTRA based on ELECTRA architecture and employed similar hyperparameters as described by Clark et al. [277] except for the batch sizes. They employed batch sizes 1024 for ELECTRA base and 4096 for ELECTRA large models and compared performance with existing BERT healthcare models. ELECTRA employs diverse coding techniques similar to other transformer models to optimize performance. These include token embeddings for converting input tokens into dense vectors, positional encoding to maintain text sequence order, and replacement token detection where the discriminator distinguishes original from substituted tokens. It handles various types of text data, making it versatile for NLP tasks such as text classification (e.g., sentiment analysis, spam detection, topic categorization), question answering, language translation, named entity recognition, and text summarization. ELECTRA’s efficient pre-training mechanism enhances accuracy and speed across these applications.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EMI-RFIAndTemperatureExtremes
:EMI-RFIAndTemperatureExtremes rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazards ;
                               :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DataCorruption;ServiceDisruptions> ;
                               :HasDetectability :MediumToHighDetectability ;
                               :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                               :HasMitigation :MaintainingStableEnvironmentalConditions ,
                                              :RedudantCoolingSystems ,
                                              :ShieldingCriticalEquipmentFromInterference ;
                               :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#NearbyDevices;InadequateControls> ;
                               :HasSeverity :MediumToHighSeverity ;
                               :HasDetectabilityScore 4 ;
                               :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                               :HasSeverityScore 7 ;
                               rdfs:comment """Electromagnetic interference (EMI), radio frequency interference (RFI), and extreme temperatures can significantly impact the reliability and performance of healthcare IT systems, potentially leading to data corruption and hardware failures. EMI and RFI often originate from nearby electronic devices or industrial equipment, while temperature extremes may result from inadequate environmental controls. These factors can cause data errors, hardware malfunctions, and system shutdowns. Mitigation strategies include shielding critical equipment from interference, maintaining stable environmental conditions, and using redundant cooling systems to prevent overheating, thereby ensuring IT infrastructure reliability and preserving data integrity.

Environmental factors can critically affect the performance and safety of medical devices. EMI can disrupt critical medical equipment an applications, potentially leading to failures with high severity in diagnostic or treatment processes. RFI can cause similar disruptions as EMI, affecting the accuracy and reliability of medical devices with medium to high severity, depending on the sensitivity of the medical equipment being used. Cadence PCB solutions mentioned in their report, as EMI can harm critical applications, risk assessments need to include hazard assessments and assessments of hazard probabilities. They also noted that a combination of standards and design best practices needs to be employed to reduce risks as complexity increases. The occurrence of these environmental factors can vary greatly depending on geographical location, local infrastructure, and the specific technologies employed within healthcare settings. Risk increases with the complexity of the environment and the proximity of devices to each other. Detecting EMI and RFI, which are typically medium to high, usually requires specialized equipment and monitoring systems, often implemented in healthcare environments.""" .


