###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FDM
:FDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :ImageData ,
                  :VideoData ;
     :UsesTechnique :FourierTokenMixers ,
                    :MultiStageDiffusionBlocks ,
                    :SpectralConvolution ,
                    :TemporalFeatureExtraction ;
     rdfs:comment "The Fourier Diffusion Models (FDM) utilize Fourier-based techniques to address specific video action segmentation and image enhancement issues. The Diffused Fourier Network (DFN) leverages the spectral domain for efficient temporal dependency capture and refines action segmentation through multi-stage diffusion, minimizing computational load while improving accuracy. The FourierDiff model employs Fourier priors for zero-shot image enhancement and deblurring, decomposing amplitude and phase information to improve image brightness and sharpness without relying on paired training data. DFN addresses image deblurring and video action segmentation challenges, such as capturing long-range dependencies and mitigating over-segmentation from noisy frame-level predictions. The authors utilized Fourier Token Mixers to transform video frames into the spectral domain for efficient temporal feature extraction and applied Multi-Stage Diffusion (MSD) models to iteratively refine segmentation results. They evaluated their model on Georgia Tech Egocentric Activities (GTEA), 50salads, and Breakfast datasets, and they employed metrics like frame-wise accuracy, segmental edit score, and F1 scores at various tIoU thresholds. They implemented their model with the Adam optimizer (learning rate: 0.0005, batch size: 1), which includes a Visual Feature Encoder (VFE) with 5 Fourier Token Mixer blocks per stage and MSD with 100 denoising steps, achieving superior while maintaining lower computational complexity and fewer parameters. FDMs support image and video data and can employ several techniques, such as Fourier token mixers to mix tokens over temporal windows in video sequences, multi-stage diffusion blocks for iterative denoising and refinement through diffusion processes, temporal feature extraction to capture long-term dependencies in video frames, and spectral convolution to enhance computational efficiency, to enhance the models’ performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FTL-GM
:FTL-GM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
        :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                       :CORAL ,
                       :ImageData ,
                       :OneHotEncoding ,
                       :StructuredData ,
                       :TextData ;
        rdfs:comment "The Federated Transfer Learning Generative Model (FTL-GM) aims to leverage the advantages of both federated learning and transfer learning to enhance the training of generative models in a decentralized manner. These models allow multiple clients to collaboratively train a global model on their local data without sharing the raw data, preserving privacy. The trained global model can then be fine-tuned using transfer learning techniques to improve performance on specific tasks for individual clients. The purposes of FTL-GM include data privacy preservation, efficient knowledge transfer across domains, and the ability to handle data scarcity by generating synthetic data that mimics the distribution of real data. These models are particularly useful in scenarios like industrial quality prediction, healthcare, and other fields where data privacy and personalization are critical. To address data privacy issues and personalized model training in industrial scenarios where data is distributed across isolated data islands, the authors in Ref. [308] proposed ACGAN-FTL framework to connect federated learning with transfer learning via ACGAN. Federated learning trains a global model across multiple clients, and ACGAN generates synthetic data for transfer learning to build personalized models. This framework combines federated learning with ACGAN, where the global model is fine-tuned using synthetic data. They trained their developed model on batch sizes 128 for federated learning and 256 for transfer learning by employing the Adam optimizer with learning rates of 0.001 for federated learning and 0.0001 for transfer learning. They improved accuracy and data privacy preservation in quality prediction for pre-baked carbon anodes. FTL-GM supports various data types, including image, text, and structured data, depending on the application scenarios and can employ One-Hot encoding for nominal data, batch normalization, and CORAL for domain adaptation in transfer learning." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FURL
:FURL rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
      :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                     :ImageData ,
                     :TemporalEnsembling ;
      rdfs:comment "Federated Unsupervised Representation Learning (FURL) leverages the vast amount of unlabeled data distributed across edge devices to learn common representation models without supervision while ensuring data privacy. The primary purpose of FURL is to  enable the learning of high-quality representations that can be used for various downstream tasks (such as classification, clustering, and anomaly detection) in a privacy-preserving manner. FURL addresses the challenges of data distribution shifts (non-IID data) and misalignment of representations across clients using techniques like federated contrastive averaging with dictionary and alignment modules. The main challenges in FURL include inconsistency of representation spaces and misalignment of representations. Due to non-IID data among clients, local models may focus on different categories, leading to inconsistent representation spaces. Without unified information among clients, representations across clients can be misaligned, negatively affecting the global model’s performance. The authors in Ref. [314] introduced the Federated Contrastive Averaging with Dictionary and Alignment (FedCA) algorithm to tackle these challenges. FedCA combines Dictionary and Alignment modules with contrastive learning to train local models, ensuring that similar samples are mapped to nearby points in the representation space. The process involves clients training local models using their data, generating local dictionaries, and then the server aggregating these models and dictionaries into a global model and dictionary. The experiments were conducted using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001, a weight decay of 0.000001, and a batch size of 128. These hyperparameters were used across various datasets like CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and MiniImageNet to train models for 100 epochs. The dictionary size was set to 1024, the momentum term of temporal ensembling was 0.5, and the scale factor for the alignment loss was 0.01. FURL primarily supports image data and utilizes batch normalization and temporal ensembling techniques to ensure consistent representation updates across training rounds." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FZSL
:FZSL rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
      :HasModality :AudioData ,
                   :ImageData ;
      :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                     :DataAnonymisation ,
                     :DataAugmentation ,
                     :DifferentialPrivacy ,
                     :FederatedAveraging ,
                     :UnsupervisedClustering ;
      rdfs:comment """Federated Zero-Shot Learning (FZSL) integrates the concepts of zero-shot learning with federated learning to recognize new, unseen classes in a decentralized, privacy-preserving manner. The main purpose of FZSL is to allow the global model to identify and learn from new classes that appear on individual client devices without having direct access to the local data. This approach enhances the model’s ability to generalize and adapt to new data distributions while maintaining user privacy. It is particularly useful in scenarios where new categories of data emerge frequently, such as audio classification, visual recognition, and other applications involving heterogeneous data. Authors designed a framework for synthesizing anonymized data impressions from class similarity matrices. They classified two scenarios for label acquisition and handling statistical heterogeneities across devices to handle new/unseen classes in federated learning settings for audio classification without data transfer. They trained their model on the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size of 32. They conducted experiments on different datasets, such as Google Speech Commands and UrbanSound8K, across multiple federated learning iterations, simulating homogeneous and heterogeneous scenarios. Authors proposed a federated zero-shot learning method for visual recognition. They combine federated learning with zero-shot learning techniques by utilizing auxiliary information to learn representations for new classes to recognize new visual categories in a federated setting where data privacy is crucial. They trained the FZSL model on the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size 64 and performed experiments on visual datasets to evaluate the performance. Authors addressed the fairness and statistical heterogeneity in federated learning by proposing Federated learning with Zero-shot Data Augmentation at the Clients (Fed-ZDAC) and Federated learning with Zero-shot Data Augmentation at the Server (Fed-ZDAS) for zero-shot data augmentation. They employed zero-shot data generation based on shared models, followed by data augmentation at the client or server. They trained their model on SGD with a momentum optimizer at a learning rate of 0.01 and batch size of 128. They evaluated Fed-ZDAC and Fed-ZDAS methods on datasets such as MNIST, Fashion MNIST (FMNIST), and CIFAR-10 to demonstrate improvements in fairness and accuracy. FZSL supports audio and image data and utilizes several techniques such as batch normalization, unsupervised clustering to differentiate new classes, data anonymization for class similarity matrices, federated averaging,
data augmentation, and differential privacy.""" .


