###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FeatureImportanceDifference
:FeatureImportanceDifference rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                      :UtilityMetrics ;
                             rdfs:comment "In the TSTR approach, the performance of the algorithm is of key importance. However, in many practical settings, the features on which the algorithm relies for predictions are invaluable. This provides insight into which features influence the outcome the most. For this reason, it may be interesting to investigate whether features’ importance in predictions when an algorithm is trained on synthetic or real data are similar." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FeatureMatching
:FeatureMatching rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FeatureWisePlotsAndStatistics
:FeatureWisePlotsAndStatistics rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :VisualSimilarity ;
                               rdfs:comment "To determine whether univariate feature distributions of synthetic and real data are similar, feature-wise plots like histograms, boxplots, or kernel density plots can be provided next to descriptive statistics like mean and covariance. Additionally, bivariate plots and statistics can be provided to investigate whether relationships between individual features are retained." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Fed-Trans
:Fed-Trans rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                    :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
           :HasModality :AudioData ,
                        :ImageData ,
                        :TextData ,
                        :TimeSeriesData ;
           :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                          :EmbeddedFLTechniques ,
                          :FrontLN ;
           rdfs:comment "Federated Transformers (Fed-Trans) combines the strengths of Transformer architectures with the federated learning paradigm. These models enable decentralized training of large-scale transformer models across multiple clients or devices without sharing raw data, thus preserving privacy and security. The primary purposes of federated transformers are to enhance model performance by leveraging distributed data, ensure data privacy, reduce communication overhead, and maintain computational efficiency. They are particularly useful in applications such as text-to-speech synthesis, anomaly detection, adversarial training, and human activity recognition. To overcome the privacy concerns and communication costs in Human Activity Recognition (HAR), authors introduced a lightweight transformer with a federated learning framework, TransFed, for HAR classification. They combine the advantages of transformers, federated learning, and lightweight model design and train their model on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 32 to improve HAR performance. Moreover, the authors in Ref. [313] Proposed a federated adversarial training framework for transformers to ensure robustness and privacy. They integrated adversarial training techniques with federated learning. They trained their model on the Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0001 and a batch size of 64 to enhance model robustness and privacy. Fed-Trans supports text, image, audio, and time series data as well as can employ Front Layer Normalization (Front-LN) to improve convergence stability, batch normalization, and embedded FL privacy-preserving techniques." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Fed3DGAN
:Fed3DGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
          :HasModality :ImageData ,
                       :VideoData ;
          :UsesTechnique <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#3DGaussianSplatting> ,
                         :AttentionModules ,
                         :BatchNormalisation ,
                         :GradientPenalty ;
          rdfs:comment "Federated 3D GAN (Fed3DGAN) aims to enable the collaborative training of GANs on 3D data across multiple decentralized devices or institutions while preserving data privacy. These models are particularly useful in medical imaging and other fields requiring 3D data synthesis and segmentation. The primary purpose of Federated 3D GANs is to combine the power of GANs for generating realistic 3D data with federated learning to ensure that sensitive data remains local and private. By doing so, they enhance data diversity and model robustness while maintaining the privacy and complying with data protection regulations. The authors in Ref. [318] developed a federated learning pipeline using Wasserstein GAN with Gradient Penalty (WGAN-GP) to generate synthetic brain MRI data and improve segmentation models. They integrated 3D-UNet with attention modules for effective brain tumor segmentation while ensuring data privacy and dealing with data insufficiency. They trained the Federated Learning with Wasserstein Generative Adversarial Network (FLWGAN) model using Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0002 and batch size of 16. They conducted experiments on the Medical Segmentation Decathlon (MSD) dataset for brain tumor segmentation tasks and demonstrated state-of-the-art performance. Authors introduced a scalable federated learning framework for 3D Gaussian splatting to tackle high computational costs and privacy concerns in3D Gaussian splatting for large-scale 3D data synthesis and rendering. The developed Fed3DGS model enhances the performance and privacy preservation on large-scale 3D datasets through distributed training on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size of 32. Fed3DGAN can handle Large-scale 3D data, including volumetric medical imaging. It can employ several techniques such as batch normalization, gradient penalty to ensure stable training and high-quality data generation, attention modules to enhance segmentation accuracy by focusing on relevant features, and 3D Gaussian splatting for efficient 3D data synthesis and rendering." ,
                       "Supports 3DData" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedCGAN
:FedCGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
         :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                            :TabularData ,
                            :TextData ;
         :UsesTechnique :ConditionalBatchNormalisation ,
                        :OneHotEncoding ;
         rdfs:comment "Federated Conditional Generative Adversarial Networks (FedCGAN) enhance privacy in federated learning by leveraging Conditional GANs (CGANs) to generate synthetic data that preserves privacy while maintaining model performance. Unlike traditional federated learning methods, which may expose sensitive data through gradients or model updates, FedCGAN decomposes each client’s network into a private extractor and a public classifier. The extractor remains local, protecting privacy, while generators are shared with the server to aggregate shared knowledge and enhance the performance of local networks. FedCGAN employs specific training methodologies and hyperparameters to ensure effectiveness and stability. For instance, learning rates typically range from 0.000001 to 0.001, and batch sizes vary depending on the dataset and task requirements. Optimization is commonly performed using Adam or AdamW optimizers, which are well-suited for handling the stochastic nature of GAN training. To address the challenge of averaging model gradients due to data multimodality at multiple nodes, authors proposed a method where multiple nodes share a synthetic version of data to hide patient-specific visual cues, with a central node responsible for training a deep model for medical image classification. They used a GAN-based aggregation strategy to generate synthetic data, learning the original data distribution, projecting private samples into the GAN latent space, clustering these samples, and generating synthetic images by interpolating cluster centroids. This technique minimized the risk of latent vector collision and sensitive information leakage. Tested on two X-ray datasets for Tuberculosis classification with Adam optimizer (learning rate 0.000001, batch size 64), the approach yielded performance comparable to or better than training on the full joint dataset.  Authors developed a secure and collaborative framework, FedCSCD-GAN, for clinical cancer diagnosis using federated learning and GAN optimization. By leveraging distributed data sources, the framework aims to improve cancer diagnosis accuracy while maintaining security for sensitive patient data. They identified QuasiIdentifiers (QIden) as independent attributes in health data, classifying others as confidential information (CI). F-differential privacy anonymization was applied to QIden attributes to enhance security and privacy, and the resulting data was mixed with CI attributes. The Cramer GAN was then trained on anonymized data using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size of 32, utilizing Cramer distance for efficiency. Experimental analysis showed their architecture accurately detects cancer while addressing security and collaboration issues. FedCGAN uses several techniques like One-Hot encoding for categorical data and conditional batch normalization to generate coherent, class-specific features. Combined with adversarial training, these techniques ensure that the synthetic data maintains the statistical properties of the real data, resulting in high-quality, privacypreserving datasets. FedCGAN can handle image, text, and tabular data, making it versatile for various federated learning applications, particularly in healthcare, where privacy concerns are critical and synthetic data generation supports research without compromising patient confidentiality." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedCSGM
:FedCSGM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
         :HasModality :ImageData ;
         :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                        :ClusteringBasedPrototypeModeling ,
                        :ConstractiveLearning ,
                        :VAEEncoding ;
         rdfs:comment "Federated Cross-Silo Generative Model (FedCSGM) enhances the collaborative capabilities of federated learning across different domains, such as healthcare, finance, and education, by operating under the federated learning framework while maintaining rigorous privacy standards. This approach utilizes cross-silo configurations, where a limited number of entities with substantial computational resources participate, enabling effective collaboration without sharing raw data. The core mechanism of FedCSGM involves using advanced cryptographic techniques and differential privacy to ensure that data remains confidential and secure during the learning process. By allowing entities to maintain local control over their data and only share model updates, these models significantly mitigate risks associated with data breaches and privacy violations. FedCSGM facilitates the generation of synthetic, privacy-preserving data, which can be used for training and enhancing machine learning models without compromising the integrity of the original data.  Authors developed a Federated learning Distributed Information Sharing (FedDIS) model to address the common issue of non-Independent and Identically Distributed (non-IID) data across medical institutions. Their approach involved training VAE to encode local medical images into a hidden space, estimating the distribution information of this hidden space, and sharing this information among clients to generate new data locally. The VAE and task models (Visual Geometry Group 16 (VGG16) for MRI classification and a CNN for MNIST classification) were trained with Adam optimizer on a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size 64, with periodic model updates aggregated using the FedAvg algorithm across multiple communication rounds. Their method preserved patient privacy while significantly improving the performance of federated learning on non-IID datasets. Similarly, authors introduced a cross-silo prototypical calibration model, which involves learning data patterns via clustering and using augmented contrastive learning to align features from different sources into a unified space and effectively mitigate the negative impact of data heterogeneity, resulting in better model generalization and performance. They trained their model on SGD optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01 and batch size of 64, and they conducted experiments on datasets including CIFAR10, CIFAR100, TinyImagenet, and VireoFood172, with each client training locally for 10 epochs per communication round. FedCSGM mostly handles image data and employs several techniques, such as augmented contrastive learning for aligning features from different clients, clustering-based prototype modeling for generating class-aware prototypes, VAE encoding for encoding local medical images into a hidden space, and batch normalization to speed up training and reduce communication costs." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedDG
:FedDG rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
       :HasModality :ImageData ;
       :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                      :DataAugmentation ,
                      :EpisodicLearning ,
                      :InstanceNormalisation ,
                      :RegularisationTerm ;
       rdfs:comment "Federated Domain Generalization (FedDG) aims to improve the performance of machine learning models on unseen domains by leveraging decentralized data from multiple clients. This approach addresses domain shift issues by training a global model that generalizes well to new, unseen domains. FedDG is particularly useful in federated learning scenarios where data privacy and decentralization are crucial, such as medical image analysis. It ensures that the global model is robust and effective across diverse domains without requiring direct access to all client data. There is always a challenge of domain shift in machine learning models when tested on unseen domains, as well as the privacy risks and communication costs in federated learning. To address these challenges, authors introduced a model, gPerXAN, which combines instance normalization and batch normalization with a guiding regularizer to enhance generalization ability while maintaining privacy in federated learning. Their method involves personalized normalization schemes and explicit differential mixtures. They trained their model on different datasets, such as PACS, Office-Home, and Camelyon17, by employing an SGD optimizer with a learning rate of 0.002 to showcase the model performance. Authors developed a model involving episodic learning in continuous frequency space, where clients share information to improve model generalization. Their approach includes self-supervised learning techniques and domaininvariant feature extraction. They validated their model on medical image segmentation tasks by employing an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size of 32, which revealed improved performance and generalization. FedDG is specifically designed for image data and employs several techniques, such as batch normalization, instance normalization to discard domain-specific visual styles while retaining domain-invariant features, regularization term to capture domain-invariant representations directly, episodic learning to simulate domain shifts and improve generalization, self-supervised learning to ensure models learn from unlabeled data, metalearning to adapt models to new domains by simulating domain shifts during training. Data augmentation to expose models to a broader range of artificially generated domains." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedDis
:FedDis rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
        :HasModality :ImageData ;
        :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                       :GammaShift ,
                       :MetaLearning ,
                       :ShapeConstistencyLoss ;
        rdfs:comment "Federated Disentangled Representation Learning (FedDis) is designed to collaboratively train an unsupervised deep convolutional neural network for data segmentation without sharing local data. Its primary purpose is to address the challenge of statistical heterogeneity across different medical domains by disentangling the parameter space into global (shape) and local (appearance) parameters. This approach leverages shared anatomical structures while keeping client-specific contrast information private, enhancing anomaly detection performance, such as in brain MRIs. Medical imaging data is often scattered across multiple organizations, each with different data distributions due to various acquisition parameters and device manufacturers. Traditional centralized learning methods are infeasible due to privacy regulations and data accessibility issues. Authors introduced FedDis, a self-supervision technique to enforce healthy reconstructions, mitigate domain shifts, and improve anomaly detection. The training process includes a loss of shape consistency and latent orthogonality to enforce disentanglement. They conducted various experiments on multiple datasets from different institutions by employing the Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0001 and a batch size of 8. Models were trained for 50 rounds, each consisting of 5 local epochs. FedDis is specifically designed for image data and employs several techniques, such as batch normalization, latent orthogonality loss to ensure the disentanglement of shape and appearance parameters, shape consistency loss to ensure shape embeddings remain consistent under different augmentations, self-supervision to clean the dataset and enforce healthy reconstructions, and Gamma shift for data augmentation during training." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedFSLGM
:FedFSLGM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
          :HasModality :TextData ,
                       :TimeSeriesData ;
          :UsesTechnique :BERTBasedEncoding ,
                         :BatchNormalisation ,
                         :ImageData ,
                         :TemporalEmbedding ;
          rdfs:comment "The Federated Few-Shot Learning Generative Model (FedFSLGM) aims to address the challenge of learning effective models with limited labeled data in a federated learning setting. These models utilize GANs and transformer-based architectures to create synthetic data or augment existing datasets, thus enabling robust learning from limited examples. The primary purpose of these models is to improve the generalization and accuracy of machine learning models across decentralized data sources while ensuring data privacy and security, especially in fields like healthcare where data is sensitive and often scarce. Foundation models face significant challenges in scenarios with limited labeled data and high data diversity. Due to data privacy concerns and variability across institutions, these challenges include difficulties in applying federated learning techniques to medical image analysis. Additionally, leveraging small datasets in medical applications using transformer-based models presents substantial obstacles. To overcome the limitations of federated learning with foundation models, authors integrated generative learning with foundation models in a federated setting to improve the robustness and generalization through generative data augmentation. They employed an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size of 32 and validated their model on diverse datasets, demonstrating significant performance gains. Authors incorporated advanced image processing and generative techniques to enhance model performance, specifically for medical image analysis. Their developed generative model employed an SGD optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01 and batch size of 64 to augment data and improve model robustness. They validated their model on extensive medical image datasets and demonstrated improved accuracy and privacy preservation. Authors developed a semi-supervised transformer-based model for few-shot learning, CEHR-GAN-BERT, for both in-cohort and out-of-cohort data in an adversarial setting. They combined transformer-based models with GANs and trained the model on an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size of 32 for both labeled and unlabeled data in an adversarial setting to improve few-shot learning. They demonstrated state-of-the-art performance on few-shot learning tasks in healthcare. FedFSLGM supports text, image, and time series data as well as can employ several coding techniques, such as batch normalization to stabilize and speed up training, temporal embeddings to incorporate time-based information, and BERT-based encoding for robust text representation to enhance the model’s performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedGM-EC
:FedGM-EC rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
          :HasModality :GraphData ,
                       :ImageData ,
                       :TabularData ,
                       :TextData ;
          :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                         :DataAugmentation ,
                         :GenerativeCodingTechniques ;
          rdfs:comment "Federated Generative Models for Edge Computing (FedGM-EC) aims to leverage distributed data across various edge devices to train generative models without compromising data privacy and security. These models are designed to generate synthetic data or enhance existing datasets, improving machine learning tasks’ overall performance and robustness while reducing the need for centralized data aggregation. The purpose is to ensure efficient data processing and learning in decentralized environments like the Internet of Things (IoT) and mobile edge devices, where data privacy and communication costs are critical concerns. Federated Generative Models for Edge Computing face several challenges, including maintaining data privacy and reducing communication costs while generating high-quality synthetic data. Additionally, handling distributed data generation from diverse sources in IoT environments and addressing missing data issues are significant concerns. Enhancing federated learning performance on heterogeneous mobile edge devices also presents a notable difficulty. To overcome the difficulty of handling distributed data generation from diverse sources in IoT environments, authors proposed a hierarchical federated generative model framework for IoT systems that follow synchronous and asynchronous updating strategies for efficient training. The framework includes a three-layer hierarchical structure with community generators at edge servers and a global generator at the cloud server. They trained their model for various datasets by opting for a batch size 32 with a learning rate of 0.01 for SGD and 0.0001 for Adam. They demonstrated the effectiveness of their model on simulated and real datasets. To address the missing data issue and enhance federated learning performance on heterogeneous mobile edge devices, the authors developed a model to generate synthetic data to augment incomplete datasets and improve learning outcomes on edge devices. They employed an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size of 64 to train their model and demonstrated significant improvements in model accuracy and data completeness. FedGM-EC supports a variety of data, including image, text, audio, video, and time series, as well as can employ batch normalization to stabilize training, generative coding techniques for filling missing data, and data augmentation to improve data quality." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedGNNs
:FedGNNs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
         rdfs:comment "Federated Graph Neural Networks (FedGNNs) are designed to enable collaborative learning on graphstructured data across multiple decentralized devices or institutions without sharing sensitive data. The primary purpose of FedGNNs is to leverage the rich relational information in graph data while maintaining data privacy and security. These models are particularly useful when graph data is distributed and privacy regulations prevent data centralization, such as in healthcare, finance, and social networks. FedGNNs utilize federated learning techniques to aggregate knowledge from local models trained on individual graphs, thus improving the overall model performance without direct data exchange. Applying GNNs in a federated learning setup presents challenges like data privacy, communication efficiency, and model performance. Additionally, there is a lack of standardized benchmarks for evaluating federated learning algorithms on GNNs. Incomplete data and privacy concerns further complicate the use of federated learning for disease prediction with GNNs. Moreover, the non-IID nature of data in federated learning for EHRs adds to these challenges. Authors introduced FedGraphNN, a benchmark framework for federated GNNs, which includes various federated learning algorithms and standardized datasets to benchmark the performance of federated GNNs. They employed several hyperparameters, varied by algorithm tested, typically including Adam optimizers, learning rates around 0.001 to 0.0001, and batch sizes of 32. Authors proposed FedNI, a federated learning framework that includes network inpainting to handle incomplete graphs. Their model used a two-phase federated learning approach: network inpainting with GANs to predict missing nodes and edges, followed by federated GCN training for node classification. They trained the model on Adam optimizer for medical datasets with a learning rate 0.001 and showed improved performance over traditional methods. Authors developed a personalized federated graph learning framework to handle non-IID data. They utilized meta-learning techniques within a federated learning framework to adapt the model to non-IID EHR data. The approach includes personalized training on local graphs and global aggregation. They trained their developed model on Adam optimizer for HER data with a learning rate 0.0001 and demonstrated improved model accuracy on EHR datasets. FedGNNs support image, text, tabular, and graph data, as well as can employ differential privacy, secure multi-party computation, batch normalization, dropout for regularization, and meta-learning for personalizing the federated model to enhance the model’s performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedMMGAN
:FedMMGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
          :HasModality :ImageData ,
                       :StructuredData ,
                       :TextData ;
          :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                         :FederatedAveraging ,
                         :SGDWithMomentum ;
          rdfs:comment "Federated Multimodal GAN (FedMMGAN) enables collaborative learning on multimodal data across decentralized devices or institutions without sharing sensitive data. These models utilize GANs to generate synthetic multimodal data, including images, text, audio, and other data types. The primary purpose of Federated Multimodal GANs is to improve the quality and diversity of synthetic data generated while maintaining data privacy and security. This is particularly useful in fields like healthcare, where multimodal data (e.g., medical images and text reports) is common, and privacy is paramount. In the healthcare environment, it is always difficult to generate accurate medical reports from multimodal data while ensuring data privacy and synthesizing high-quality medical images across different modalities in a privacy-preserving manner. Authors implemented a multimodal federated learning framework that combines visual and textual data to generate medical reports. Their approach includes local training on individual datasets and global model aggregation using the federated averaging algorithm. They trained their developed model on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.00005 and batch size of 16 and employed other optimization techniques to enhance model performance and privacy. Authors utilized a federated learning framework with GANs for unsupervised domain translation between different medical image modalities. The training involves local model updates and global aggregation to synthesize high-quality images. They trained their model on a batch size 4 with Adam optimizer, which had a learning rate of 0.0001 and demonstrated improved image synthesis quality and privacy preservation. FedMMGAN supports various data types, including text, images, and structured data. It can implement batch normalization, federated averaging for model parameter aggregation, and SGD with momentum for efficient model optimization to enhance the model performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedRL
:FedRL rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
       :HasModality :ImageData ,
                    :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :AttentionMechanisms ,
                      :BatchNormalisation ,
                      :FederatedAveraging ,
                      :TemporalDifference ;
       rdfs:comment "Federated Reinforcement Learning (FedRL) integrates federated learning with reinforcement learning to enable decentralized training of Reinforcement Learning (RL) models across multiple devices or institutions without sharing raw data. The primary purpose of FedRL is to enhance learning efficiency and model performance in environments where data privacy and security are paramount, such as healthcare and dynamic data systems. It leverages the advantages of both federated learning (privacy-preserving data integration) and reinforcement learning (dynamic decision-making and optimization) to handle non-IID data and improve training stability and timeliness in federated settings. To address the performance degradation in federated learning with non-IID data, the atuhors introduced a modular architecture for federated learning with reinforcement learning policies to optimize aggregation strategies. They conducted experiments by employing an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 using the Federated Extended-MNIST (EMNIST) dataset with CNN for image classification. They demonstrated improved performance with a reinforcement learning policy for non-IID data aggregation. Additionally, for urban security, the authors optimized the timeliness and freshness of data in dynamic acquisition systems. They developed a multi-agent deep reinforcement learning algorithm with an attention mechanism for computing offloading and resource allocation. They used federated learning to train agents with a batch size of 128 on Adam optimizer with learning rates of 0.001 for Actor and 0.002 for Critic across mobile terminals and edge base stations. FedRL commonly supports image and time series data, as well as can employ various techniques, such as federated averaging, batch normalization, temporal difference, and attention mechanism, to enhance data efficiency and security." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FedVAE
:FedVAE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :FederatedLearningBasedGenerativeModels ;
        :HasModality :ImageData ,
                     :TextData ,
                     :TimeSeriesData ;
        :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                       :DataAugmentation ,
                       :DifferentialPrivacy ,
                       :Dropout ;
        rdfs:comment "Federated Variational Autoencoders (FedVAE) enable privacy-preserving and collaborative learning across decentralized data sources without sharing raw data. They combine the principles of federated learning with variational autoencoders (VAEs) to achieve efficient and effective training on distributed datasets. The primary purpose of FedVAE is to allow multiple clients to train a global model while keeping their data local collaboratively, thus preserving privacy. This approach is particularly useful in scenarios where data privacy is paramount, such as in healthcare, collaborative filtering for recommender systems, and IoT applications. Maintaining data privacy and handling the heterogeneity of IoT data present significant challenges, alongside the need for privacy-preserving recommender systems that do not compromise performance. To overcome such challenges, authors iemployed the FedVAE framework to collaboratively train models on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size of 32 for IoT healthcare data while preserving privacy. Their approach includes federated learning protocols and variational autoencoders to handle the data distribution and ensure privacy. Authors developed FedVAE for top-N item recommendation in a federated learning setup. They implemented an adaptive learning rate schedule to accelerate training and enhance stability. Their model uses a federated learning framework to train a variational autoencoder on a learning rate of 0.001 for Adam optimizer, with a batch size 32 for most datasets, for collaborative filtering. FedVAE supports image, text, and time series data, as well as can implement batch normalization, data augmentation to enhance the robustness of the model, dropout for regularization and differential privacy, and model compression to reduce communication overhead to sustain the model performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FederatedAveraging
:FederatedAveraging rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             :Techniques .


