###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FewShotGAN
:FewShotGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :GANs ;
            :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                               :TextData ,
                               :TimeSeriesData ;
            :UsesTechnique :AdversarialAutoencoders ;
            rdfs:comment """Few-Shot GAN (FSGAN) is a cutting-edge generative model that excels in producing high-quality synthetic images from limited data samples. This capability is particularly valuable in fields like healthcare, where data scarcity is common. FSGAN employs advanced generative techniques to effectively learn and mimic complex data distributions with minimal input. It is designed with a strong focus on transparency and reproducibility, featuring comprehensive documentation of its architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics, and supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data. Few-Shot GAN typically uses learning rates between 0.00001 and 0.002 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence. Due to data shortage, FSGAN often utilizes smaller batch sizes ranging from 4 to 32. Authors trained their model on Adam optimizer by employing learning rate 0.001 and batch size 32 to address the problem of few-shot classification, the authors in Ref. [168] proposed adaptive metalearning approach for FSGAN and trained their model with SGD and Adam optimizer on MNIST dataset by
employing learning rate 0.0002 and 0.00001 with batch size 4. Moreover, authors discovered the Adam optimized pruning method for few-shot learning with GAN data augmentation by varying learning rates, between 0.0001 and 0.002, on Residual Network (ResNet) 34.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FilteringOrDenoisingTechniqueLog
:FilteringOrDenoisingTechniqueLog rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                           :NoiseHandlingMechanismsClaimEvidence .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FineGAN
:FineGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :GANs ;
         :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                            :TextData ,
                            :TimeSeriesData ;
         :UsesTechnique :AdversarialAutoencoders ,
                        :BatchNormalisation ,
                        :FeatureMatching ,
                        :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ,
                        :NoiseInjection ;
         rdfs:comment "FineGAN refines the traditional GAN architecture by introducing hierarchical decompositions within the generator, allowing for the creation of distinct and controllable synthetic images that capture and replicate complex visual hierarchies. This hierarchical approach ensures that FineGAN can produce images with varying detail and structural complexity levels, making it particularly effective for generating detailed and diverse synthetic data. FineGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data. It is designed with a strong emphasis on reproducibility and transparency. Its framework is thoroughly documented, providing step-by-step training procedures and robust evaluation metrics. It typically employs a learning rate of around 0.0002 for stabilizing the training process and ensuring convergence, such as authors used Adam optimizer, set the learning rate 0.0002, and employed a batch size 16 to train their two-stage generation model for unsupervised fine-grained image generation. Similarly, authors employed the same hyperparameters for fine-grained class clustering. Moreover, authors employed the Adam optimization method to update the generator and the discriminators at a learning rate of 0.0002 and optimize the classifier by using SGD with the learning rate of 0.001 and used the class-conditional and hyper-spherical GANs technique for fine-grained image synthesis." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FisherDivergence
:FisherDivergence rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :Techniques .


