###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FrechetInceptionDistance-FID
:FrechetInceptionDistance-FID rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                       :EffectivenessMetrics ,
                                       :FidelityMetrics ,
                                       :RealismMetrics ,
                                       :StatisticalDistanceMeasures ;
                              :HasModality :ImageData ;
                              rdfs:comment "The Fréchet Inception distance is commonly used to assess image quality from generative models. It fits multivariate Gaussian distributions to image representations (usually computed through Inception v3) and computes the Fréchet distance between them. Fréchet distance considers not only the distance between points but also their ordering, whereby it penalizes large deviations and favors representations that maintain smooth alignment with the ground truth. This makes it especially fit for image data. Fréchet distance is also called Wasserstein-2 distance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FrontLN
:FrontLN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FullySynthetic
:FullySynthetic rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         :DataProportion ;
                rdfs:comment "Fully synthetic data is data entirely generated by a data generating model. It contains no original real-world samples, although information from real-world samples can potentially leak through in the synthetic dataset. It is generated from algorithms aiming to approximate (a part of) the density function of real-world data, after which synthetic samples can be randomly drawn from this approximated density. These randomly drawn samples are ideally untraceable to real-world individuals used in training the algorithm. Fully synthetic data often aims to mimic the variations and complexity observed in real-world use cases. Fully synthetic data can especially tackle data scarcity issues when too little real-world data is available or when it is too sensitive to be used. Resampling methods, statistical models, and machine learning models are common methods for generating fully synthetic data." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/FullySyntheticData
:FullySyntheticData rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                    rdfs:comment "Fully synthetic data is data entirely generated by a data generating model. It contains no original real-world samples, although information from real-world samples can potentially leak through in the synthetic dataset. It is generated from algorithms aiming to approximate (a part of) the density function of real-world data, after which synthetic samples can be randomly drawn from this approximated density. These randomly drawn samples are ideally untraceable to real-world individuals used in training the algorithm. Fully synthetic data often aims to mimic the variations and complexity observed in real-world use cases. Fully synthetic data can especially tackle data scarcity issues when too little real-world data is available or when it is too sensitive to be used. Resampling methods, statistical models, and machine learning models are common methods for generating fully synthetic data." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/GAEs
:GAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Graph Autoencoders (GAEs) are an effective tool for learning efficient laten representations of graph data, facilitating tasks such as link prediction, clustering, and visualization. These models are designed to clearly focus on transparency and reproducibility, providing well-documented methodologies that detail their architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics. GAEs typically employ a learning rate between 0.01 and 0.00001 for stabilizing the training process and ensuring convergence, authors trained their model by employing Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01 to shape the optimization for aerodynamic using graph variational autoencoders and genetic algorithms. Authors employed variational graph autoencoders and trained their model on Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 to 0.00001 and a batch size 256, and found the lowest training loss, to generate synthetic EHRs. GAEs are highly versatile and can handle a variety of structured data types, making them applicable across different domains and as well as employ several techniques, such as Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs), Variational Graph Autoencoders (VGAEs), and Graph Attention Networks (GATs) to improve the model performance." .


