###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InaccurateDataAnalysis
:InaccurateDataAnalysis rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :TabularDataHazards ;
                        :HasConsequence :MisguidedDecisionsClinicalErrors ;
                        :HasModality :TabularData ;
                        :HasPotentialCause :AlgorithmBiasesIncorrectModeling ;
                        :HasDetectabilityScore 8 ;
                        :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                        :HasSeverityScore 8 .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InaccurateDataProcessing
:InaccurateDataProcessing rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                   :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ,
                                   :PatientReportedData ;
                          :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#MisleadingClinicalInsights;PatientHarm> ;
                          :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                          :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
                          :HasMitigation :ContinuousMonitoringOfDataProcessingWorkflows ,
                                         :RobustTestingAndValidationAlgorithms ,
                                         :ThoroughDocumentation ;
                          :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#FaultyAlgorithms;SoftwareBugs> ;
                          :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                          :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
                          :HasLikelihoodScore 8 ;
                          :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                          rdfs:comment """Inaccurate data processing occurs when errors are introduced during the transformation of raw data into a usable format. These errors can arise from faulty algorithms, incorrect data handling, or software bugs, leading to misclassifications, incorrect estimations, and ultimately, misleading clinical insights. Causes include software bugs, misapplication of algorithms, and human errors in setting up processing pipelines. Consequences range from misleading clinical insights to potential patient harm due to incorrect diagnoses or treatments. Mitigation strategies include robust testing and validation of algorithms, continuous monitoring of data processing workflows, and thorough documentation to prevent errors.

Inaccurate data processing can lead to significant misdiagnoses, incorrect treatment plans, and poor patient outcomes. For instance, errors in big data analytics used for healthcare diagnostics can propagate wrong conclusions, which may have life-threatening consequences. This has been emphasized in Anjum, M., et al.’s study that explores error reduction and the organization of big data in healthcare systems. They mentioned that big data analytics eliminates unwanted raw data from the database, and healthcare applications contain various datasets that require proper analysis processes to enhance the performance range of the systems. They also mentioned that medical health records maintained in the healthcare system contain various datasets that require proper processing and analysis. Given the complexity of healthcare datasets (structured and unstructured), the likelihood of inaccurate data processing remains moderate to high, especially in environments where data is collected from disparate sources without robust validation mechanisms. Detecting inaccuracies in data processing is challenging and typically requires continuous validation and error-checking processes. Detectability is often low unless systematic audits or anomaly detection systems are in place.""" .


