###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateDataBackupAndRecovery
:InadequateDataBackupAndRecovery rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          :DataStorageAndManagement ,
                                          :OperationalData ;
                                 :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ExtendedDowntime;LossOfOperationalDataAndContinuity> ;
                                 :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                                 :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                                 :HasMitigation :AutomatedBackupSystem ,
                                                :BackupStorageInMultipleLocations ,
                                                :IntegrityBackups ;
                                 :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#TechnicalFailures;InsufficientBackupFrequency> ;
                                 :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                                 :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                                 :HasLikelihoodScore 5 ;
                                 :HasSeverityScore 10 ;
                                 rdfs:comment """Inadequate data backup and recovery refer to failures in regularly backing up data or using unreliable methods that don’t ensure data can be restored. Several studies indicate that inadequate backups can lead to prolonged downtime and data loss, significantly impacting healthcare operations. The consequences of technical failures and insufficient backup frequency include irrecoverable data and extended downtimes. Effective strategies involve implementing automated backup systems, storing backups in multiple locations, and regularly testing the integrity of backups.

The failure to effectively back up and perform data recovery can result in significant data loss with critical implications for patient care and compliance with health regulations. Its impact can be very high, as discussed in Open Access Government news about the COVID-19 pandemic, which resulted in an immediate demand for large-scale data modeling and visualization to track spikes in cases. This dramatic increase in data being written and read requires a scalable file storage system to store large data sets, underscoring the critical need for robust and scalable backup and recovery systems to handle sudden increases in data usage, which impacts the severity of inadequate practices. The likelihood and detectability can be medium, depending on the organization’s adherence to best practices in data management and the robustness of their IT infrastructure.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateDataNormalisation
:InadequateDataNormalisation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                      :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                             :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SkewedInsights;MisleadingDecisions> ;
                             :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                             :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                             :HasMitigation :CarefulPreprocessingSteps ,
                                            :ConsistentDataNormalisationProtocols ;
                             :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InconsistentFormats;LackOfPreprocessing> ;
                             :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                             :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                             :HasLikelihoodScore 8 ;
                             :HasSeverityScore 8 ;
                             rdfs:comment """Inadequate data normalization leads to incorrect analysis due to the failure to standardize data before processing. This issue can result in skewed insights and misinterpretation of clinical data. Literature highlights causes including inconsistent data formats, improper handling of outliers, and lack of preprocessing, with consequences of distorted analysis and misleading clinical decisions. Mitigation involves implementing consistent data normalization protocols and careful preprocessing steps to ensure data accuracy and comparability.

Incomplete or inadequate data normalization carries a high severity rating, as it directly affects the accuracy of the data used for making critical healthcare decisions. The 4medica report highlighted that poor data quality in healthcare can have profound implications for patient well-being and satisfaction. When data is inaccurate, incomplete, or inconsistent, it can lead to incorrect diagnoses, inappropriate treatments, and unnecessary medical tests. Such mistakes can lead to avoidable health complications and adverse outcomes, which reflects the high severity of inadequate data normalization. The likelihood of occurrence is typically medium, reflecting the challenges inherent in handling diverse and voluminous healthcare data. The detectability of normalization issues can be low, especially when stringent quality checks and audits are not in place.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateEncryption
:InadequateEncryption rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               :DataSecurityAndPrivacy ;
                      :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ExposureOfSensitiveData;IncreasedBreachRisk> ;
                      :HasDetectability :LowToMediumDetectability ;
                      :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                      :HasMitigation :CrossCheckedPractises ,
                                     :CryptographicProtections ,
                                     :EncryptionAlgorithms ,
                                     :SectorSpecificRequirements ;
                      :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InadequateResourceAllocation;FlawedDocumentation> ;
                      :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                      :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27799Standard> ,
                                   <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Regulation2017/745Standard> ,
                                   :CyberSecurityInMedicalDevicesStandard ,
                                   :DICOMStandard ,
                                   :HealthLevel7FHIRStandard ,
                                   :IEC81001-5-1Standard ,
                                   :IHEProfilesStandard ,
                                   :NISTAES-256Standard ,
                                   :TLS1.3Regulation ;
                      :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
                      :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                      :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                      rdfs:comment """Inadequate encryption refers to outdated or flawed encryption methods that fail to protect data during storage or transmission, making it vulnerable to unauthorized access. Literature highlights causes such as outdated encryption algorithms and poor key management. This can result in exposure of sensitive data and increased risk of data breaches. Mitigation strategies include adopting strong encryption algorithms such as AES-256 or post-quantum standards, ensuring secure key management, and regularly updating encryption practices to counter emerging threats. Cryptographic protections are based on NIST AES-256 and TLS 1.3, and can be implemented within an ISO/IEC 27001-governed information security management system. Sector-specific requirements can be addressed via IEC 81001-5-1 (health software security) and ISO/IEC27799 (information security management in health), while interoperability layers reference HL7 FHIR, IHE profiles, and DICOM to maintain secure, auditable exchange. In addition, practices can be cross-checked against the FDA Cybersecurity in Medical Devices guidance and MDR 2017/745 to ensure technical controls underpin regulatory compliance.

Inadequate encryption exposes healthcare data to the risk of interception and unauthorized access, compromising patient confidentiality and organizational integrity, which underscores the severity of inadequate encryption as high. Shojaei, P., et al. discussed the importance of strong encryption techniques throughout
the data lifecycle in mobile health applications. They mentioned that data security can be addressed as early as the design phase, such as when integrating security policies, defining the sensitivity levels of form fields, and implementing corresponding security mechanisms. Although encryption technologies are well-known, their implementation can be inconsistent, which signifies the likelihood concern is medium. Sargiotis D. described the implementation of encryption as \"critical,\" yet acknowledged the variable adherence to best practices in different systems or settings. He also emphasized that detecting inadequate encryption is inherentlydifficult, which marks the detectability as low to medium, as breaches might not be immediately apparent until a security incident occurs.""" .


