###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncorrectDataEntry
:IncorrectDataEntry rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             :DataEntryAndCollection ;
                    :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Misdiagnoses;IncorrectTreatmentPlans> ;
                    :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                    :HasMitigation :AutomatedDataCapture ,
                                   :ErrorCheckingMechanisms ;
                    :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ManualErrors;SystemMalfunctions> ;
                    :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                    :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                    :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                    :HasSeverityScore 8 ;
                    rdfs:comment """Errors during data entry, whether manual or automated, are a significant hazard. Manual data entry is particularly prone to errors such as typographical mistakes, incorrect coding, and selection errors in dropdown menus. Automated data capture, though designed to minimize errors, can still introduce inaccuracies due to sensor malfunctions, incorrect calibration, or data synchronization failures. These errors can lead to misdiagnoses, incorrect treatment plans, and delays in patient care, as supported by studies highlighting the cascading effects of initial data inaccuracies on clinical outcomes.


Incorrect data entry is a significant concern in healthcare, particularly in systems where manual data entry is prevalent. The severity of this issue is high, as errors in data entry can result in incorrect diagnoses. An ECRI Institute report on data errors in health IT systems reveals that human-computer interface issues are the most frequent cause of data entry errors, accounting for 65% of health IT-related incidents. Minor errors can escalate into severe patient safety risks if undetected. The likelihood of these errors being medium to high, as mentioned in the Institute for Healthcare Improvement (IHI) report. ’If the detection of failure takes too long, it may become too late to repair the situation, and the magnitude of the problem will become much greater’. However, the likelihood of these errors is heightened in busy environments, with detectability varying but is often medium depending on the presence of cross-checks or validation tools in place.""" .


