###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/K-ValueLog
:K-ValueLog rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :ComplianceEvidence ,
                     :VerificationLogic ;
            :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC20889Standard> ;
            rdfs:comment "> = threshold ^ 1 > = threshold for verification logic." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KAnonymity
:KAnonymity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :PrivacyModels ,
                     :MetricThreshold ,
                     :PrivacyAgainstInference ;
            :AttributeCategory :Quasi-Identifiers ;
            :HasModality :TabularData ,
                         :TimeSeriesData ;
            :HasRoleCompliance :BaselineProtectionAgainstSinglingOut ;
            :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC20889Standard> ;
            :ApplicableToTypesOfValues "All" ;
            :DataTruthfulnessAtRecordLevel "NO" ,
                                           "YES" ;
            :HasMetricValue """For Group-Based Privacy Models

k>=5 (minimum) - Low baseline protection
k>=10 - Standard protection
k>=20 - High protection""" ;
            rdfs:comment "For k-anonymity, “Yes” applies unless k-anonymity is implemented via microaggregation (in which case truthfulness is “No”)." ,
                         "Measures the minimum size of equivalence classes sharing the same quasi-identifiers" ,
                         "Similar to differential privacy, k-anonymity was developed as a privacy-preserving framework and originates in. The general idea is that a synthetic dataset provides k-anonymity if a particular record for an individual in the synthetic dataset is indistinguishable from at least k-1 records also appearing in the synthetic dataset, which ensures that inference on specific individuals is hampered. K-anonymity is regarded as a more traditional technique for ensuring the privacy of individuals with some serious weaknesses, and after the ϵ-differential privacy framework was developed in 2006, its use decreased sharply. Variants of k-anonymity were developed because of its shortcomings, such as K-map privacy and I-Diversity." ,
                         "k-Anonymity, as articulated in clause 10.2, examines the size of equivalence classes defined by quasi-identifiers, requiring that each such class contain at least k records to limit the probability of unique linkage. ISO/IEC 20889 illustrates, through annexed examples, how small k-values or heterogeneous distributions can render this protection ineffective in the presence of adversaries with auxiliary information. Within k-anonymity is implemented and primarily applied should use diagnostically in the pre-synthesis evaluation of raw datasets, in order to assess the size of equivalence classes defined by quasi-identifiers and to identify high-risk attribute combinations prior to model training. Post-synthesis, rather than relying on k-anonymity of the synthetic records themselves, the framework gives preference to measures such as k-map, which estimate the identifiability of real records as measured through the synthetic dataset. This approach is methodologically more appropriate in the synthetic-data context, as it focuses on the residual disclosure risk affecting original data subjects, rather than on the internal anonymity properties of the synthetic records alone.and the post-synthesis evaluation of synthetic clusters that may be too narrow or statistically revealing." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KDE
:KDE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :ParametricModels .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KLDivergence
:KLDivergence rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       :StatisticalDistanceMeasures ;
              rdfs:comment """KL divergence is a measure that quantifies the difference between two probability
distributions. The authors in use approximate KL divergence to compare the output of models for synthetic data generation. It is not a proper metric as it is asymmetric, meaning that the distance between distribution 1 and 2 is not necessarily equal to the distance between distribution 2 and 1. KL divergence is often used to train generative models. Here, we can distinguish forward KL divergence as the divergence between the actual and learned distribution and reverse KL divergence as the divergence between the learned and actual distribution. Forward KL divergence favors learning distributions covering the full support of the actual distribution, whereas reverse KL divergence favors learning distributions concentrated in high-likelihood areas of the actual distribution. Which of the two is favorable depends on the task at hand.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KLDivergenceScaling
:KLDivergenceScaling rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KMap
:KMap rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :PrivacyAgainstInference ;
      rdfs:comment "The k-map privacy metric can be seen as a relaxed version of k-anonymity and originates from. Here, third parties are assumed not to know the original real dataset. They only know of an external reidentification dataset containing the original real dataset. Then, k-map is satisfied when at least (k-1) similar records are contained in the reidentification dataset for each synthetic record. It is important to note that k-map privacy only specifies the conditions a dataset must adhere to to ensure privacy but does not describe a specific algorithm for implementation. Here, it also applies that there are relatively few implementations of k-map privacy in current work in synthetic data generation in a health context after the Differential Privacy framework was developed." .


