###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KulpersTest
:KulpersTest rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      :StatisticalTests ;
             rdfs:comment """Similar to the aforementioned tests, Kuiper’s test is a nonparametric two-sample
test. It is computed by adding the maximum positive and maximum negative difference between two empirical cumulative distribution functions. This way, the test is similarly sensitive to differences in the center and tails of distributions as the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test but is invariant under cyclic transformations. This makes the test more useful for periodic data such as time-ofday or seasons""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/L-ValueLog
:L-ValueLog rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :ComplianceEvidence ,
                     :VerificationLogic ;
            :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC20889Standard> .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LDM
:LDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :AudioData ,
                  :ImageData ,
                  :TextData ,
                  :VideoData ;
     :UsesTechnique :CrossAttention ,
                    :NoiseScheduling ,
                    :TemporalAttention ;
     rdfs:comment "Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) serve various purposes across various domains by working in a compressed latent space to reduce computational costs. In text-to-audio generation, LDMs create high-quality audio from text, facilitating creative audio synthesis. LDMs transform image models into video generators for highresolution video generation, supporting applications like autonomous driving simulations and personalized content creation. In medical imaging, LDMs generate realistic 3D brain images, enhancing research datasets and ensuring data privacy. The authors in Ref. [355] designed AudioLDM, a Text-To-Audio (TTA) system, to generate high-quality audio from text descriptions with improved efficiency. They addressed the limitations of previous TTA systems, which required large, high-quality paired datasets. AudioLDM leverages LDMs and contrastive languageaudio pretraining (CLAP) embeddings to bypass the need for paired data. Their methodology includes CLAP for joint audio-text embeddings, a VAE to compress mel-spectrograms, and a UNet-based LDM for the generation process, incorporating techniques like mixup augmentation and classifier-free guidance. They conducted experiments on different datasets, such as AudioSet and AudioCaps, to evaluate the performance using metrics such as Frechet Distance and Inception Score. The model was optimized using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0000045 and a batch size of 6. Latent Diffusion Models also support text, image, audio, and video data. It utilizes several techniques, such as noise scheduling and iterative denoising processes to transform text into audio data, temporal attention for 3D convolutions, and hybrid conditioning approaches combining concatenation and cross-attention mechanisms, to enhance models’ performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LDP-GAN
:LDP-GAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :GANs .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LDiversity
:LDiversity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :MetricThreshold ,
                     :PrivacyAgainstInference ;
            :HasModality :TabularData ;
            :HasRoleCompliance :AttributeDisclosureMitigation ;
            :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC20889Standard> ;
            :HasMetricValue """For Group-Based Privacy Models

l>=2 - Minimal diversity
l<3-5 - Adequate protection""" ;
            rdfs:comment "L-diversity, in turn, addresses the weakness of k-anonymity in cases where equivalence classes are homogeneous with respect to sensitive attributes. The standard demonstrates with numerical examples that inferential disclosure may occur even when quasi-identifiers are shared across several individuals, such as in medical datasets where certain attributes (e.g., diagnosis codes) exhibit strong correlations." ,
                         "L-diversity, originating in, was developed as an improvement over k-anonymity. It proposes a stronger privacy guarantee by ensuring diversity of sensitive information within the indistinguishable groups from k-anonymity. L-diversity requires at least L distinct values of a sensitive feature within each group. When there is sufficient diversity within these groups, sensitive attribute inference on individuals is hampered. Due to identified weaknesses in L-diversity, like K-anonymity and K-map privacy, the differential privacy framework superseded the use of these more traditional techniques in synthetic data generation." ,
                         "Measures diversity of sensitive attributes within k-anonymous groups." ,
                         """l>=2 - Minimal diversity
l>=3-5 - Adequate protection""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LSGAN
:LSGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TabularData ,
                          :TextData ;
       :UsesTechnique :FeatureMatching ,
                      :NoiseInjection ;
       rdfs:comment "Least Squares GAN (LSGAN) modifies the traditional GAN architecture by integrating a least squares loss function. This adjustment aims to minimize Pearson X2 divergence, stabilize the training process and enhance the quality of generated synthetic images by reducing the likelihood of mode collapse. LSGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and tabular data. LSGAN is designed to comprehensively focus on transparency and reproducibility, offering detailed documentation of its architecture, training procedures, hyperparameter settings, and data processing techniques. It typically utilizes learning rates in the range of 0.0001 to 0.1 for the stable and convergent training process with commonly used batch sizes ranging from 8 to 100 to balance computational efficiency with model performance. It can be trained using various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD. Authors compared deep convolution and least squares GANs for diabetic retinopathy image synthesis. They kept the batch size constant, 64, and varied other hyperparameters, such as learning rates, 0.01, 0.003, 0.0002, and optimizers, such as Adam, RMSProp, and SGD for DRI, Oxford, and CelebA datasets. Authors used Adam optimizer, kept the batch size 64, and tuned the anime cartoon character dataset on different learning rates, such as 0.001, 0.002, 0.005, and 0.01, to demonstrate the performance of DC-GAN vs LS-GAN. Authors employed Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and a batch size of 100 to train the model What Not to Learn, and authors investigated image compression by utilizing the Adam optimizer with a fixed learning rate, 8, and varied the learning rates from 0.0001 to 0.00001." .


