###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LatencyInDataTransmission
:LatencyInDataTransmission rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                    :DataTransmissionAndSharing ;
                           :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DelayedDecisions;ImpactedCare> ;
                           :HasDetectability :HighDetectability ;
                           :HasMitigation :OptimizingNetworkInfrastructure ,
                                          :RealTimeDataProcessing ,
                                          :UtilizingEdgeComputing ;
                           :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#NetworkCongestion;BandwidthLimitations> ;
                           :HasSeverity :MediumSeverity ;
                           :HasDetectabilityScore 3 ;
                           :HasLikelihoodScore 5 ;
                           :HasSeverityScore 6 ;
                           rdfs:comment """Delays in data transmission, known as latency, can significantly impact healthcare delivery, particularly in emergencies. Literature highlights that latency issues often stem from network congestion, bandwidth limitations, or processing bottlenecks. It can delay critical clinical decisions. Strategies to reduce latency include optimizing network infrastructure, implementing real-time data processing, and utilizing edge computing to shorten data travel times.

Delays in the transmission of healthcare data can impact the timeliness and efficiency of patient care, particularly in critical care settings. The severity and likelihood of these errors are often medium, as network issues, system overloads, and the increasing volume of data being transmitted can contribute to delays. A discussion about the challenges in health information exchange in the Global Threat Landscape Report highlighted difficulties in securing transmissions, indirectly pointing to latency issues as a part of broader transmission challenges in healthcare data management. The detectability is high, as latency issues are usually apparent to users experiencing delays in data access or communication.""" .


