     :HasModality :TabularData ,
                  :TimeSeriesData ;
     rdfs:comment "Comparison of temporal correlation - Timeseries" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MAGVIT
:MAGVIT rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
        :HasModality :ImageData ,
                     :VideoData ;
        :UsesTechnique :ClassConditionalGeneration ,
                       :FramePrediction ;
        rdfs:comment "Masked Generative Video Transformers (MAGVIT) employ a transformer-based architecture with masked modeling techniques to generate high-fidelity, diverse synthetic videos across various visual domains. These models excel in producing detailed and contextually accurate visual content, making them particularly valuable for applications like healthcare MRI imaging and complex scene reconstructions. Similar to MaskGIT, MAGVIT was developed by Google researchers to address various video synthesis tasks with a single model. They introduced a 3D tokenizer to convert a video into spatial-temporal visual tokens and proposed an embedding method for masked video token modeling to support multi-task learning. They trained MAGVIT to generate 16-frame videos at 128×128 resolution, using a 3D-vector-quantized model that converts a video into 4×16×16 visual tokens with a visual codebook size of 1024. They evaluated MAGVIT’s single-task video generation performance on three benchmarks: class-conditional generation on UCF-101, frame prediction on BAIR Robot Pushing with a 1-frame condition, and frame prediction on Kinetics-600 with a 5-frame condition. They quantitatively evaluated MAGVIT on BAIR and SSv2 for multi-task video generation across 8-10 tasks. The experiments showed that MAGVIT surpasses existing methods in inference time, being two orders of magnitude faster than diffusion models and 60 times faster than autoregressive models. Additionally, one model can handle ten diverse generation tasks and generalize across various video domains.." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MC-WGAN
:MC-WGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :GANs .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MCDM
:MCDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :DiffusionModels ;
      :HasModality :ImageData ,
                   :TextData ;
      :UsesTechnique :GuidedDiffusion ,
                     :Langevin ,
                     :ScoreMatching ;
      rdfs:comment "The Manifold-Constrained Diffusion Model (MCDM) addresses optimization problems with unknown constraints by learning the feasible data space and using a two-stage sampling process involving guided diffusion and Langevin dynamics. Diffusion models can adapt to low-dimensional manifold structures, avoiding dimensionality issues by focusing on intrinsic data dimensions. The Manifold-Constrained Classifier Free Guidance (CFG++) improves text guidance in diffusion models by tackling off-manifold problems, enhancing sample quality and consistency. Denoising diffusion models effectively use manifold structures to generate diverse samples supported by theoretical convergence results. For image synthesis, manifold guidance in diffusion models ensures unbiased and high-quality image generation by aligning samples with the true data manifold. To address the challenge of optimizing real-world problems where analytic objective functions or constraints are unavailable, the authors in Ref. [358] proposed DIFFOPT. This two-stage sampling methodology ensures optimization within the feasible data manifold learned from diffusion models. The first stage involves a guided diffusion process to warm up and initialize samples on the manifold, followed by a Langevin dynamics stage for further refinement. Experiments were conducted on synthetic and real-world datasets, including blackbox optimization and multi-objective molecule optimization. Key hyperparameters include a learning rate of 0.001, a batch size of 128, and specific annealing strategies for the inverse temperature. The experimental setup utilized a 2-layer Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) with 1024 hidden dimensions for the score network and employed Variance Preserving SDE for the forward process. The MCDM supports text and high-resolution image data types, as well as utilizes guided diffusion for initialization, Langevin dynamics for accurate sampling, and score matching for modeling diffusion processes." .


