###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MaskGIT
:MaskGIT rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
         :HasModality :ImageData ;
         :UsesTechnique :LayerNormalisation ,
                        :PotionalAndProgressiveEmbedding ,
                        :TruncatedNormalInitialisation ;
         rdfs:comment """Masked Generative Image Transformers (MaskGIT) use a transformer-based architecture with masked modeling techniques to generate high-fidelity, diverse synthetic images. These models produce detailed and contextually accurate visual content, making them valuable for applications requiring high-quality synthetic datasets, such as healthcare imaging and complex scene reconstructions.
Researchers from Google recently introduced this model, which is a new image synthesis method using a bidirectional transformer decoder. MaskGIT predicts randomly masked tokens by attending to tokens in all directions during training. During inference, the model generates all image tokens simultaneously and iteratively refines the image based on the previous generation. They used the Adam optimizer with β1=0.9 and β2=0.96, set label smoothing and dropout rate to 0.1, and trained all models (ImageNet and Places2) with a batch size of 256. For data augmentation, they employed RandomResizeAndCrop during decoding. They evaluated MaskGIT on standard class-conditional image generation tasks on ImageNet and demonstrated its versatility in three image editing tasks: inpainting, outpainting, and editing. MaskGIT model can employ several techniques, such as positional and progressive embedding, Layer Normalization, and truncated normal initialization, to enhance the model performance. This model can be extended to various image editing tasks, such as inpainting, extrapolation, and image manipulation.""" .


