###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MaxPooling
:MaxPooling rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MaxRTSSimilarity
:MaxRTSSimilarity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :RecordSimilarity ;
                  rdfs:comment "Maximum Real-to-synthetic (Max-RTS) similarity computes the similarity of all generated synthetic vectors (or parts of them) with a smaller, random sample of real vectors (or parts of them), for example, with cosine similarity. Average Real-to-Real (RTR) similarity between samples of real data is typically also computed as a reference value for the RTS. Similar RTS and RTR values indicate that the real and generated data have the same underlying distribution, which may indicate overfitting and privacy issues. Max-RTS is a more general measure that computes the maximum similarity between all generated and real vectors (or parts of both), and in this way, allows identification and omission of synthetic vectors that are very close to real vectors, as specified by a pre-defined similarity threshold. Vectors above the threshold could be copies of the training data. RTS-similarity has been employed as a privacy measure in work generating tabular cardiovascular, diabetes, and obesity data" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MaximumLikelihoodEstimation-MLE
:MaximumLikelihoodEstimation-MLE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          :UtilityMetrics .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MaximumMeanDisrepancy-MMD
:MaximumMeanDisrepancy-MMD rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                    :FidelityMetrics ,
                                    :StatisticalDistanceMeasures ;
                           :HasModality :TimeSeriesData ;
                           rdfs:comment "Many divergence measures can become computationally expensive in high dimensions. Maximum mean discrepancy has been developed to quantify differences between probability distributions while being efficient in high dimensions. The method embeds data in a high-dimensional Hilbert space through a chosen kernel (i.e., Gaussian) and compares the means of these embeddings. These embeddings can capture higher-order interactions, after which a simple estimate like the mean provides more information on distributional similarity than in the original feature space. The maximum mean discrepancy has been used widely in assessing generative models." .


