###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Megatron-LM
:Megatron-LM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
             :UsesTechnique :LayerNormalisation ,
                            :MultiHeadSelfAttention ,
                            :PositionalEncoding ,
                            :TextData ,
                            :TokenEmbedding ;
             rdfs:comment "Megatron-LM optimizes transformer-based language models to efficiently handle extensive data, significantly enhancing performance in language modeling and text generation tasks. Megatron-LM is assembled on the transformer architecture, leveraging multi-head self-attention mechanisms and position-wise feedforward networks to capture complex dependencies in the data. It is designed to scale effectively, accommodating very large models with billions of parameters. Large-scale text datasets can be preprocessed using tokenization, normalization, and batching to ensure consistency and quality. It typically sets the learning rate between 0.00001 and 0.00015 for stable and efficient convergence and uses large batch sizes benefiting from distributed training strategies that leverage multiple Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) or Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) to handle vast amounts of data simultaneously. It employs advanced optimizers such as Adam and LAMB (Layer-wise Adaptive Moments optimizer for Batch training) to minimize loss functions effectively. Authors proposed an intralayer model parallel approach that enables training transformer models with billions of parameters. For GPT-2 models, they performed training with sequences of 1024 subworlds at a learning rate of 0.00015 and batch size 512, and for BERT models, they used the original BERT dictionary with a vocabulary size of 30522 and employed batch size of 1024 and learning rate of 0.0001 to train the model to replace the sentence prediction head with sentence order prediction. Megatron-LM can handle various data types and is highly effective for language modeling, text generation, translation, and summarization tasks. It can be adapted for tasks involving structured text data, such as JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) or Extensible Markup Language (XML), making it suitable for data extraction and organization tasks, as well as applicable for recognizing clinical concepts, medical question answering, assessing semantic textual similarity, and natural language inferences. Megatron-LM employs techniques such as token embeddings, positional encodings, multi-head self-attention, and layer normalization to enhance model performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MembershipDisclosure
:MembershipDisclosure rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
