                      rdfs:comment "In tabular data: statistical bias, bias-eliminated coverage, power,empSE, privacy (Membership disclosure - F1 score) as a universal framework for assessing replicability." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MembershipInferenceAttack-MIA
:MembershipInferenceAttack-MIA rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :PrivacyMetrics ;
                               :HasModality :TimeSeriesData .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MembershipInferenceRisk
:MembershipInferenceRisk rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  :PrivacyAgainstInference ;
                         :HasModality :TabularData ,
                                      :TimeSeriesData ;
                         rdfs:comment "Another potential privacy risk emerges when an attacker has some information concerning a particular individual, e.g., an EHR obtained due to a data leak, and uses that information to infer that the individual was in the train data with which a particular generative model was trained. Such cases are more generally known as Membership Inference Attacks (MIA), and by inferring membership, the third party may even learn new information about that individual if the training dataset the individual is in, concerns, for example, specific diseases. It is argued that in realistic MIA settings, the attacker has at least some information about the individual in question, like a (partial) EHR, i.e., the individual is known to the attacker a priori. In the case where an attacker has no access to external data, he may query a generative model and compare the output with a set of real samples. Then, if the model overfits the real dataset, the attacker may be able to infer which of the real samples were likely used in training the generative model. MIA can be a first step in disclosing further sensitive information, such as attributes or profiles. Measures for controlling Membership Inference Risk have been frequently employed in generating EHRs." .


