###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MoE
:MoE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
     :HasModality :AudioData ,
                  :ImageData ,
                  :TabularData ,
                  :TextData ;
     :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                    :EmbeddingLayers ,
                    :LayerNormalisation ,
                    :OneHotEncoding ;
     rdfs:comment "The Mixture of Experts (MoE) model enhances performance across diverse tasks by integrating multiple specialized models, each contributing its unique strengths to the overall system. MoE employs a gating mechanism to dynamically select and combine outputs from various expert models, optimizing the model’s performance for specific tasks. This approach is particularly effective in handling complex and heterogeneous data, making MoE a versatile solution in various domains. MoE’s training involves concurrent training of multiple expert models, each focusing on different aspects of the data. The gating mechanism coordinates the experts, selecting the most relevant ones for each input. MoE models typically use learning rates that vary across experts, such as the authors in Ref. [281] developed Mixture-of-Experts for Biomedical Question Answering (MoEBQA) model to alleviate the parameter competition problem that decouples the computation for different types of questions by sparse routing. For MedQA, they used a pre-trained PubMedBERT model and employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.005 and a batch size of 16, as well as employed the same pre-trained model for HEAD-QA with a learning rate of 0.00005 and batch size of 8 to retrieve the relevant document. They also used the RoBERTa-large model to train the NLPEC dataset on Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.00002 and batch size 16 and observed the improvement in accuracy of RoBERTa-large by 7.4 MoE models are adept at processing various data types, leveraging the strengths of specialized experts for each type. Transformer-based experts are particularly effective for text generation, sentiment analysis, and language translation tasks. Experts can also handle structured data, such as tabular datasets, using techniques like feature engineering and embedding layers to capture relational information. For multimedia data, such as images, audio, and video, MoE can integrate CNNs or RNNs as experts to extract relevant features and patterns. MoE employs flexible coding schemes tailored to the specific needs of each expert model. This flexibility allows MoE to handle a wide range of data types effectively. Experts often utilize transformers, which excel in capturing long-range dependencies in data. This is particularly useful for tasks involving NLP and understanding complex sequences. For categorical data, one-hot encoding is frequently used to convert labels into a binary matrix format, making it easier for the models to process and learn from categorical information. For text data, embedding layers are used to convert words or tokens into dense vector representations, capturing semantic relationships and context within the data. Batch or layer normalization can be applied to stabilize and accelerate training, ensuring that each expert model learns efficiently." .


