###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/OverfittingAndUnderfittingOfModels
:OverfittingAndUnderfittingOfModels rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                             :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                                    :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#UnreliablePredictions;PatientHarm> ;
                                    :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                                    :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
                                    :HasMitigation :BalancesDatasetsEmployment ,
                                                   :CrossValidationTechniques ,
                                                   :SimplifyingModels ;
                                    :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ComplexModels;InsufficientData> ;
                                    :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                                    :HasDetectabilityScore 6 ;
                                    :HasLikelihoodScore 7 ;
                                    :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                                    rdfs:comment """Overfitting occurs when models are too closely tailored to training data, while underfitting happens when models fail to capture underlying patterns. Both lead to poor generalization and inaccurate predictions when applied to new data. Literature indicates that overfitting is caused by overly complex models, data leakage, or excessive model tuning, while underfitting results from overly simplistic models. Consequences include unreliable predictions and potential harm to patients. Mitigation strategies include simplifying models, employing balanced datasets, and using cross-validation techniques to enhance model generalization.

Overfitting and underfitting can lead to poor generalization of machine learning models in healthcare, where the model either becomes too specific to the training data or fails to capture the underlying patterns. This can lead to misdiagnoses or inappropriate treatment recommendations, resulting in high severity. Aliferis, C.,et al. discussed this issue, as overfitting in healthcare AI can lead to model overconfidence, causing harm in decision-making, while underfitting results in models failing to capture patterns, both of which can severely impact patient outcomes. They emphasized the importance of balancing model complexity and having adequate training data to mitigate these issues. The likelihood is moderate to high, depending on the dataset and the model used. They also discussed the challenges in detecting these problems due to the technical nature of model evaluations. Typically, the detectability of overfitting and underfitting is moderate, as it can be identified through validation techniques such as cross-validation and evaluation of test-set performance.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/OverrelianceOnData
:OverrelianceOnData rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             :DataUseAndInterpretation ;
                    :HasConsequence :InappropriateClinicalDecisions ;
                    :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                    :HasLikelihood :HighLikelihood ;
                    :HasMitigation :BalancedDecisionMaking ,
                                   :ClinicalDecisionSupportSystems ,
                                   :TechnicalSupportAndEducation ;
                    :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#HighConfidenceInAlgorithms;LackOfAlternatives> ;
                    :HasSeverity :MediumToHighSeverity ;
                    :HasDetectabilityScore 6 ;
                    :HasLikelihoodScore 9 ;
                    :HasSeverityScore 7 ;
                    rdfs:comment """Overreliance on data refers to the excessive trust placed in data-driven insights, sometimes at the expense of clinical expertise and patient-specific factors, which can lead to suboptimal healthcare decisions. Literature highlights that high confidence in algorithms, organizational pressure, and lack of alternative information can result in inappropriate clinical decisions and neglect of patient preferences. Mitigation strategies involve balanced decision-making, clinical decision support systems that integrate data with clinical guidelines, and providing technical support and education to healthcare professionals on combining data with clinical judgment.

Overreliance on quantitative data can overshadow clinical judgment, leading to potential oversight of patientspecific factors that are not captured in the data, thereby affecting treatment outcomes with medium to high severity. Moreover, the increasing availability and emphasis on data-driven decisions in healthcare settings enhance the risk of overreliance, especially with the growth of electronic health records and decision-support systems. While detectability can be challenging, often rated as medium, to assess when data reliance becomes excessive, regular reviews and audits of decision-making processes can help identify and mitigate this risk. A healthcare forum ‘Safeti’ highlighted these types of risks in the blog ‘Assess the Risk | Likelihood and Severity | How to Use a Risk Matrix’, most modern health and safety legislation requires employers to assess the risk as part of their basic duties to protect their workforce.""" .


