###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PerceiverAndPerceiverIO
:PerceiverAndPerceiverIO rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
                         :HasModality :AudioData ,
                                      :ImageData ,
                                      :TextData ;
                         :UsesTechnique :LatentSpaceRepresentation ,
                                        :PositionalEncoding ,
                                        :Tokenisation ;
                         rdfs:comment "Perceiver and Perceiver IO models are advanced transformer-based architectures designed to process and generate data across multiple modalities. These models leverage scalable attention mechanisms to achieve high performance in image recognition and language understanding tasks. Their design includes detailed training methodologies and evaluation metrics, ensuring transparency and reproducibility in model development. The perceiver handles large inputs by using an asymmetric attention mechanism to distill data into a compact latent bottleneck, making it competitive with or superior to specialized models in tasks involving images, point clouds, audio, video, and combined modalities. Perceiver IO extends this versatility, scaling linearly with input and output sizes, and employs a flexible querying mechanism to perform well on diverse tasks, including natural language and visual understanding, multi-task and multi-modal reasoning, and StarCraft II, without needing task-specific designs. Perceiver models typically employ adaptive learning rates, often in the range of 0.00001 to 0.001, for stabilizing training and ensuring efficient convergence. DeepMind authors introduced the Perceiver model, trained on ImageNet and ModelNet40 for point clouds with Lamb optimizers and 512 batch sizes on learning rates 0.004 and 0.001, and exhibited the architecture competitiveness in comparison to specialized models on classification tasks across various modalities. DeepMind authors also introduced the Perceiver IO model and performed experiments on various modalities, such as for all ImageNet experiments, they employed LAMB optimizer with different learning rates, 0.002 and 0.0003, and batch size 1024, and found small learning rates are stable. This model also supports discrete modalities, such as StarCraft-II, as well as Audio, flop calculations, attention modules, language models, AutoFlow, multimodal autoencoding, etc. They also demonstrated that Perceiver IO outperforms a Transformer-based BERT baseline on the GLUE language benchmark despite removing input tokenization. It achieves progressive performance on Sintel optical flow estimation with no explicit mechanisms for multiscale correspondence. Perceiver and Perceiver IO models are capable of handling various data types, including images, text, audio, and structured data. These models effectively process high-dimensional data such as medical images (e.g., MRI, CT scans, X-rays) and perform tasks like image classification and segmentation, which are suitable for NLP tasks. They can process audio data for tasks like speech recognition and synthesis using features that capture temporal dependencies and frequency characteristics and can handle tabular data, time-series data, and other structured formats. These models can employ several coding techniques, such as tokenization, positional encoding, and latent space representations, to enhance the model performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PercentRootMeanSquareDifference-PRD
:PercentRootMeanSquareDifference-PRD rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                              :PerformanceMetrics ;
                                     :HasModality :TimeSeriesData ;
                                     rdfs:comment "Compination of accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, and ROC-AUC, to assess the model performance - for timeseries." ,
                                                  "It is used to compute the distortionbetween the original and synthetic signals." .


