###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PhysicalSecurityBreaches
:PhysicalSecurityBreaches rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                   :EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazards ;
                          :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DataTheft;SystemCompromise> ;
                          :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                          :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                          :HasMitigation :EnhancingPhysicalSecurity ,
                                         :RegularSecurityAudits ,
                                         :SecureStorage ;
                          :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InadequateSurveillance;LackOfControls> ;
                          :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                          :HasDetectabilityScore 6 ;
                          :HasLikelihoodScore 6 ;
                          :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                          rdfs:comment """Physical security breaches occur when unauthorized individuals gain access to data storage facilities or devices, potentially leading to data theft, tampering, or system compromise. Causes include insufficient physical security measures, such as inadequate surveillance or a lack of access controls. The consequences may consist of data loss, unauthorized access to data, and compromised system integrity. Mitigation strategies include enhancing physical security with biometric access controls, surveillance systems, secure storage for sensitive equipment, and regular security audits to detect and address vulnerabilities.

Physical breaches, such as unauthorized access to secure areas within hospitals, can result in theft of medical records, damage to infrastructure, or tampering with medical equipment, all of which can have severe consequences for both patient privacy and safety. Moreover, healthcare facilities often employ security measures such as access controls and surveillance systems. However, gaps in these systems, combined with human error, make physical security breaches a medium likelihood risk. Physical breaches are often detected after they occur, typically through security footage or routine security checks. However, real-time detection can be improved with more sophisticated surveillance systems and immediate alert mechanisms.""" .


