###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RAEs
:RAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Recurrent Autoencoders (RAEs) leverage RNNs to process sequential and time-series data, capturing intricate temporal dynamics. RAEs utilize RNN architectures, typically incorporating layers such as LSTM or Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) to encode sequence inputs into latent representations and decode them back, preserving the temporal properties of the data. RAEs use Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT) to optimize temporal data representations, which is essential for training RNNs, as it efficiently handles the temporal dependencies in sequential data, allowing the model to learn from long sequences. These models are particularly effective in applications such as speech recognition, music generation, and predictive maintenance, where maintaining temporal coherence is essential. RAEs commonly employed mini-batch sizes and learning rates around 0.001, authors trained their model on hyperparameters, Adam with learning rate 0.001 and batch size 64, to enhance gated recurrent unit with auto-encoder for solving text classification problems. Authors trained their model by employing several hyperparameters, including Adam, with a learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 37, to enhance the memory of the variational autoencoder for the automated music composition. RAEs mostly handle sequential data types for applications such as predictive maintenance, speech recognition, and music generation, as well as employ various techniques, including latent space representations, feature matching, and attention mechanisms, to enhance model performance and ensure the effective capture of temporal patterns." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RCAforAnomaliesContext
:RCAforAnomaliesContext rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :FailureAnalysisContext ;
                        rdfs:comment "RCA performed whenever anomalies or failures are found." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RDM
:RDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :DiffusionModels ;
     :HasModality :AudioData ,
                  :ImageData ,
                  :TextData ,
                  :TimeSeriesData ,
                  :VideoData ;
     :UsesTechnique :DDPO ,
                    :MonteCarloGradientEstimation ,
                    :RWR ,
                    :VariationalInference ;
     rdfs:comment "The Reinforced Diffusion Model (RDM) integrates generative diffusion processes with RL to enhance optimization in various tasks. In network optimization, this model improves the policy by exploring the state space more effectively, achieving better results. Additionally, it employs RL techniques in training diffusion models, specifically Denoising Diffusion Policy Optimization (DDPO), to optimize models directly for complex objectives, such as image quality and alignment, by treating the denoising process as a sequential decision-making task. This combined approach leverages the strengths of both generative models and reinforcement learning for superior performance. Authors explored training denoising diffusion models using an RL framework, Denoising Diffusion Policy Optimization (DDPO), which reframe the denoising process as a multi-step Markov Decision Process (MDP) to directly optimize complex reward functions, such as image compressibility, aesthetic quality, and prompt-image alignment, using Vision-Language Models (VLMs). The experiments were conducted using the Stabilize Diffusion v1.4 model, fine-tuning only the UNet weights and comparing DDPO to RewardWeighted Regression (RWR) methods. Key hyperparameters included in the experiment were AdamW optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-5, batch sizes of 64 for DDPOIS, 256 for DDPOSF, and 128 for RWR methods, with specific settings for weight decay, β1, β2, ϵ, and gradient clipping. Moreover, existing models struggle with compositionality and generating specified attributes. The authors in Ref. [379] developed a method, Diffusion Policy Optimization with KL regularization (DPOK), which uses RL with KL regularization to finetune models based on human feedback-derived rewards. Compared to supervised fine-tuning, this approach aims to maintain high image fidelity while enhancing text-image alignment. Experimental details include online RL training with a learning rate of 0.00001, batch size of 10 for sampling and 32 for gradient steps, using AdamW optimizer, and incorporating KL regularization weights (α = 10, β = 0.01). Supervised training used a learning rate of 0.00002, batch size of 128, and KL regularization weight (γ = 2.0). Multi-prompt training employed a learning rate 0.0001, batch size 256 for value learning, and KL regularization weight (β = 0.001). Reinforced Diffusion Models support various data types, including images, text, audio, video, and time series. These models are versatile in their application, enabling optimization and generalization across different domains by effectively exploring and utilizing the underlying data structures. These models support several techniques, such as Policy Gradient Algorithms to iteratively improve the policy in reinforcement learning tasks, Variational Inference to estimate the underlying data distributions and optimize the model, DDPO to reframe denoising as a multi-step decision-making task to apply policy gradient algorithms, RWR to weight the regression process for model optimization, and Monte Carlo Gradient Estimation for direct estimation of gradients during the optimization process." .


