###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RealTimeDataProcessingFailures
:RealTimeDataProcessingFailures rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                         :DataProcessingAndAnalysis ;
                                :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DelayedInterventions;IncorrectAlerts> ;
                                :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                                :HasLikelihood :MediumToHighLikelihood ;
                                :HasMitigation :ErrorHandlingMechanismsImprovement ,
                                               :RobustSystemInteroperability ,
                                               :SystemPerformanceOptimisation ;
                                :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemLatency;OverloadedSystems> ;
                                :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                                :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
                                :HasLikelihoodScore 9 ;
                                :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                                rdfs:comment """Real-time data processing failures occur when systems cannot handle the demands of processing data as it is generated, leading to delays or inaccuracies in responding to critical patient conditions. Several studies highlight causes such as system latency, overloaded systems, and insufficient error handling, which may lead to delayed clinical interventions and incorrect alerts. Mitigation strategies include optimizing system performance, improving error-handling mechanisms, and ensuring robust system interoperability to enhance real-time data reliability and timeliness.

These failures are considered highly severe because they can immediately threaten patient care, particularly in intensive and emergency care settings. Dash, Sabyasachi, et al. mentioned in their study on the management and usage of healthcare data, which has become increasingly dependent on information technology. The development and use of wellness monitoring devices and related software that can generate alerts and share a patient’s health-related data with their respective healthcare providers have gained momentum, especially in establishing real-time biomedical and health monitoring systems. These devices are generating a huge amount of data that can be analyzed to provide real-time clinical or medical care. This highlights the severity and immediate impact of real-time data processing failures. The likelihood is medium to high, dependent on the complexity and robustness of the IT infrastructure. Detectability might be low, as these failures can occur swiftly and without warning, requiring advanced monitoring technologies and protocols to manage effectively.""" .


