###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RedundantOrDuplicateDataEntry
:RedundantOrDuplicateDataEntry rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :DataEntryAndCollection ,
                                        :TextDataHazards ;
                               :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#MedicalErrors;IncreasedHealthcareCosts> ;
                               :HasLikelihood :HighLikelihood ;
                               :HasMitigation :CentralizedDataManagementSystems ,
                                              :RegularAudits ;
                               :HasModality :DeduplicationAlgorithmsImplementation ,
                                            :TextData ;
                               :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemAnomalies;ManualRepetition> ;
                               :HasSeverity :MediumSeverity ;
                               :HasDetectabilityScore 3 ;
                               :HasLikelihoodScore 7 ;
                               :HasSeverityScore 7 ;
                               rdfs:comment """Data redundancy occurs when the same information is entered multiple times due to system anomalies, uncoordinated data sources, or manual repetition by different staff members. This leads to duplicate records, causing confusion and inconsistencies in patient data. Consequences include increased risks of medical errors, unnecessary procedures, and inefficiencies in healthcare delivery, as documented in studies examining the impact of duplicate records on patient safety and system performance.

Duplicate entries can confuse healthcare staff, leading to delays or inappropriate treatments. The severity is moderate but can escalate depending on how quickly the error is detected. A recent Verato report highlights the impact of duplicate medical records on a patient’s record, which can cause test results or treatment history to be omitted from the record a clinician is referencing when ordering further tests or care. This can lead to repeat tests, redundant treatment, or care, and delays. At the same time, staff try to locate missing test results. They also highlighted a recent American Health Information Management Association (AHIMA) study, which showed that on average, repeated tests or treatment delays added $1,100 to the cost of the patient’s care. A RightPatient report highlighted the consequences of having duplicate records and overlays in EHR systems for
both patients and caregivers. They mentioned 20% of the medical records in healthcare systems with multiple facilities are duplicates, and they can cost up to $40 million for any healthcare provider. Moreover, the Healthcare IT Today report on ‘How to Reduce Manual Data Entry Risks in the Healthcare’ mentioned that detectability can be improved with automated checks and flagging systems, although not all systems employ these measures effectively. The likelihood is medium to high, especially in environments without realtime data synchronization or deduplication protocols.""" .


