###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Reformer
:Reformer rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
          :HasModality :ImageData ,
                       :TabularData ,
                       :TextData ,
                       :TimeSeriesData ;
          :UsesTechnique :LayerNormalisation ,
                         :PositionalEncoding ;
          rdfs:comment "The Reformer transformer model is designed to efficiently handle long sequences by utilizing innovative techniques such as reversible layers, which allows the model to backpropagate without storing intermediate activations and drastically reducing memory usage during training, and locality-sensitive hashing, which replaces the traditional dot-product attention mechanism with a hashing-based approach. These advancements significantly reduce memory requirements, making the Reformer particularly suitable for processing large datasets. The Reformer typically uses learning rates around 0.0001 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence with common batch size 8 to balance the computation and model training. Authors introduced the Visual Transformer with Feature Recombination and Distillation (ViT-FRD). This model learns from a CNN by optimizing distillation losses and incorporating two performance boosters to enhance training effectiveness and efficiency. They used a batch size of 8, trained the model with the Adam optimizer at a learning rate of 0.0001, and validated their method on two cardiac MRI image datasets.The Reformer is effective for processing various data types, particularly long documents, articles, and books, due to its efficient handling of long sequences. It is also suitable for time-series and tabular data, where capturing long-range dependencies is beneficial. It can be adapted for image data when combined with convolutional layers or other preprocessing techniques to manage spatial information. The Reformer employs various techniques such as positional encoding and layer normalization to enhance performance" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegressionModels
:RegressionModels rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :BasicMLMethods ;
                  rdfs:comment "Regression methods condition a target variable of which synthetic observations are desired on a subset of other predictor variables, for example, with linear regression models. A popular implementation of such methods is the Synthpop package, which has been used to impute synthetic data in EHRs with linear regression models and non-medical types of data with decision trees. Linear models are mainly used with tabular data and have the advantage that they are straightforward to interpret. Yet, such models may be challenging to scale up in large datasets with many features, as many independent models have to be specified. Also, since relations between variables may be non-linear and interactive, more flexible, non-parametric methods like decision trees or neural networks are typically chosen for generating data. Basic non-parametric methods are also supported by Synthpop in the form of classification/regression trees." .


