###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SAEs
:SAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are designed to learn efficient representations by enforcing sparsity constraints on the hidden layers, which enhances feature selection and reduces noise. The architecture of SAEs includes sparsity-inducing penalties, providing a structured approach to ensure a small number of active neurons in the hidden layers. This enhances the model’s ability to capture relevant features from the input data while discarding noise and irrelevant information. SAEs are typically trained at the learning rate usually around 0.01 to stabilize the training process and ensure convergence, authors employed artificial neural network and SoftMax regression based on enhanced sparse autoencoder for medical disease. They trained their model with the Softmax classifier, including a learning rate of 0.0 and a number of samples in mini-batches of 32 to demonstrate the prediction of three diseases and show the performance in diverse medical diagnoses. Authors trained the stacked sparse autoencoder-based intrusion detection model by employing an Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 1024 for connected healthcare systems. SAEs can handle various data types, including images, text, audio, and time series. SAE models are beneficial for applications like medical imaging (MRI, CT scans, and X-rays), where high-dimensional data is common and effective for anomaly detection and feature extraction in time-series data, which is suitable for applications like patient monitoring and predictive maintenance. Moreover, these models employ KL divergence, L1 penalty, and sparsity regularization techniques to enforce sparsity and improve model performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SAGAN
:SAGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :GANs ;
       :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                          :TextData ,
                          :TimeSeriesData ;
       :UsesTechnique :AttentionSelfAttentionMechanism ,
                      :BatchNormalisation ,
                      :FeatureMatching ,
                      :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ;
       rdfs:comment "Self-Attention GAN (SAGAN) leverages self-attention mechanisms to significantly enhance the quality and resolution of synthetic images. This innovative approach enables SAGAN to focus on relevant features within images, making it particularly effective for applications such as automated surveillance, advanced image enhancement, and other tasks requiring high-quality image generation. SAGAN can also support text and time series data in different use cases. SAGAN strongly emphasizes transparency and reproducibility, providing detailed documentation of its architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics. The authors in Ref. [109] trained their model on a learning rate of 0.0001 with a batch size of 64 and used RMSprop for speech enhancement. Moreover, authors detailed a description of Why Adam Beats SGD for Attention Models, and authors discussed that Adam is no better than normalized SGD by varying the learning rates and batch sizes" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SBOMCreationAndMaintenance
:SBOMCreationAndMaintenance rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                     :CyberAttacksMitigation .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SDV
:SDV rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :ParametricModels .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SGAN
:SGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :GANs ;
      :GeneratesModality :ImageData ;
      :UsesTechnique :BatchNormalisation ,
                     :FeatureMatching ,
                     :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ;
      rdfs:comment """The Stacked Generative Adversarial Network (SGAN) refines traditional GANs by utilizing a multi-layered approach with multiple generators and discriminators. This layered structure allows SGAN to generate highly detailed and realistic synthetic images by progressively refining the output through each layer. This method is particularly effective for generating high-resolution images and ensures comprehensive documentation of its architecture, training procedures, and multi-stage evaluation metrics, promoting transparency and reproducibility.
Authors experimented with photo-realistic image synthesis with SGANs by employing an Adam optimizer with a batch size of 64 and a learning rate of 0.0002. SGAN supports image, text, and mixed data, in which images are combined with text annotations or other forms of metadata.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SGDWithMomentum
:SGDWithMomentum rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SMOTE
:SMOTE rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :BasicMLMethods ;
       rdfs:comment """The Synthetic Minority Oversampling TEchnique (SMOTE) algorithm generates synthetic examples of minority classes by sampling from line segments joining a minority observation and its k-nearest neighbors. Various variations on and improvements of SMOTE have been developed; examples of variations are Support Vector Machine SMOTE (SVM-SMOTE) and K-means SMOTE that both use different underlying machine learning algorithms for identifying reference neighbors for generating data points, and Nominal-SMOTE that can also generate categorical data. An example of an improvement on SMOTE is the Adaptive Synthetic (ADASYN) sampling approach, which generates more samples for minority classes that are harder due to having fewer class neighbors. These algorithms have mostly been employed for creating samples in minority classes in imbalanced datasets so
as to improve downstream task performance, e.g., classifying the presence of diabetes in patients and catastrophic health expenditures. SMOTE is often used with tabular data but can also be used with image and audio data. Still, the use of SMOTE can pose problems as it focuses on minority classes and does not take into account the true data distribution.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SOAEs
:SOAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
       rdfs:comment "Self-organizing Autoencoders (SOAEs) combine self-organizing maps (SOMs) with autoencoder architectures to enhance unsupervised learning for dimensionality reduction and feature visualization. This hybrid model effectively maps high-dimensional data into a lower-dimensional space while preserving the topological properties of the input data. SOAEs are typically trained with adaptive learning rates ranging from 0.001 to 0.0001, which allows the model to fine-tune its parameters effectively during training. Authors performed the joint training procedure to optimize both the network parameters and the prototypes by backpropagation and stochastic gradient descent using deep embedded self-organizing maps by employing Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size 256. Similarly, authors trained deep autoencoder Gaussian mixture model using an unsupervised self-organizing map by employing a learning rate of 0.0001 and batch size of 1024 for IoT anomaly detection. They also trained and evaluated their model on internet traffic data among IoT devices to minimize false-positive rates. SOAEs are particularly effective for image compression, denoising, and clustering tasks. However, they can also support a wide range of data types, including text, image, numerical, and time series data. They mostly employ SOMs, latent space representation, and hybrid strategies. For example, SOAEs merge the competitive learning of SOMs with the loss minimization of autoencoders to enhance the quality of learned representations." .


