###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SpDM
:SpDM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :DiffusionModels ;
      :HasModality :GraphData ,
                   :ImageData ;
      :UsesTechnique :MarkovChain ,
                     :SpectralCorrelation ;
      rdfs:comment """The Spectral Diffusion Models (SpDMs) are versatile frameworks applied across various domains such as hyperspectral image classification, multispectral image reconstruction, and graph data generation. It operates by introducing Gaussian noise to the data in a forward process and removing it in reverse to capture underlying patterns. Hyperspectral imaging learns the joint latent structures to improve classification accuracy. Multispectral imaging enhances reconstruction by modeling spectral correlations and addressing wavelength mismatches. In graph data generation, it leverages the graph spectrum to focus the diffusion process on an information-dense manifold, improving both efficiency and robustness. Authors addressed the challenges of hyperspectral image classification in remote sensing, where existing deep learning methods struggle with high-dimensional and complex data. They offered a solution, SpectralDiff, which employs a generative framework using diffusion models to capture spectral-spatial distributions and relationships. Their model comprises two modules: the spectral-spatial diffusion module, which adaptively constructs sample relationships without prior graph structures, and the attention-based classification module, which enhances cross-sample perception for per-pixel classification. The methodology is validated on the Indian Pines, Pavia University, and Salinas datasets, using accuracy and kappa coefficient metrics. The diffusion model uses the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001 and a batch size of 256, while
the classification model uses a learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 64. The Spectral Diffusion Model supports various data types, including hyperspectral and multispectral images, as well as graph data, and can utilize several techniques, such as Markov chains for the forward diffusion process, spectral correlation modeling to address wavelength mismatches, and score-based modeling for efficiency and performance improvement in graph generation.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SparseEncoding
:SparseEncoding rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SpatialTransformations
:SpatialTransformations rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SpectralConvolution
:SpectralConvolution rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SpectralCorrelation
:SpectralCorrelation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/StAEs
:StAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
       rdfs:comment "Stacked Autoencoders (StAEs) consist of multiple autoencoder layers, enabling hierarchical feature extraction for improved data representation and reconstruction. The architecture of StAEs involves sequentially stacking multiple autoencoders, where the output of one layer serves as the input of the next. This structure allows for progressively more abstract and higher-level feature representations. Training is usually conducted in two phases: unsupervised pre-training, in which each autoencoder layer is trained individually in a greedy layer-wise manner to learn efficient representations, and supervised fine-tuning, in which the entire stacked network is fine-tuned using labeled data to improve the performance on the target task. Authors developed a model for human activity identification based on a smartphone motion sensor using a deep stacked autoencoder to enhance the healthcare system. They employed the default parameter documented in MATLAB 2017b and only varied the learning rate to 0.001. Moreover, StAEs support various optimizers, including Adam, RMSprop, and SGD, in training deep networks. StAEs can handle various data types, including images, text, and audio, making them suitable for a wide range of applications, as well as employs several techniques, such as greedy layer-wise pretraining, sparse encoding, denoising, and contractive encoding, to enhance performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/StableDiffusion
:StableDiffusion rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
