###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SupplyChainAndThird-PartyRisks
:SupplyChainAndThird-PartyRisks rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                         :EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazards ;
                                :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemOutages;CompromisedDataSecurity> ;
                                :HasDetectability :MediumDetectability ;
                                :HasLikelihood :HighLikelihood ;
                                :HasMitigation :DiversifyingSuppliers ,
                                               :IncorporatingContingencyPlans ,
                                               :ThirdPartyCompliance ;
                                :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#VendorFailures;LackOfCompliance> ;
                                :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                                :HasDetectabilityScore 5 ;
                                :HasLikelihoodScore 8 ;
                                :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                                rdfs:comment """Healthcare organizations often rely on third-party vendors for critical services and supplies, such as software or hardware embedding, making them vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and vendor unreliability, which can impact healthcare data systems. Literature highlights vendor failures as the primary cause of these risks. The consequences include prolonged system outages and compromised data security. Mitigation strategies involve diversifying suppliers, ensuring third-party compliance with security standards, and incorporating contingency plans for vendor-related disruptions.

Disruptions in the supply chain can severely impact healthcare operations by delaying or preventing the delivery of essential medical supplies, thereby compromising patient care. Leah Sadoian [198] highlighted the interconnectedness of digital healthcare services. She mentioned that if one system is breached, it can have ripple effects across multiple platforms and services, which highlights the severe impact that supply chain and third-party risks can have on the entire healthcare ecosystem. The interconnectedness of global supply chains and reliance on third-party vendors increase the probability of disruptions due to factors such as cyberattacks, logistical failures, or economic instability. Morgan Franklin Consulting noted that every organization relies on a network of third-party vendors, which introduces new risks to corporate cybersecurity, indicating a high likelihood of third-party risks due to dependency on external vendors. While systems are in place to monitor supply chain performance, detecting vulnerabilities within third-party operations can be challenging without rigorous and continuous auditing and risk assessment practices. Leah Sadoian also mentioned that effectively managing risks associated with third-party vendors helps maintain secure and efficient healthcare operations. Discussing management implies ongoing efforts to detect and address these risks, indicating medium detectability.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SwitchTransformer
:SwitchTransformer rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                            :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
                   :HasModality :TextData ,
                                :TimeSeriesData ;
                   :UsesTechnique :DynamicRouting ,
                                  :LayerNormalisation ,
                                  :PositionalEncoding ;
                   rdfs:comment "Switch Transformers utilize a dynamic routing mechanism that enhances their ability to process long sequences efficiently by adaptively attending to the most relevant portions of the input. This mechanism significantly improves language understanding and generation task performance, making Switch Trans highly effective for various applications in NLP and beyond. The architecture of Switch Transformer includes multiple layers of attention mechanisms, where each layer dynamically routes subsets of the input through different experts (sub-networks) based on their relevance. This dynamic routing reduces computational costs by selectively processing parts of the input, thereby enhancing the model’s efficiency and scalability. Despite the notable successes of MoE, its widespread adoption has been hindered by complexity, communication costs, and training instability. To address these issues, authors simplified the MoE routing algorithm and developed improved models with reduced communication and computational costs. Their proposed training techniques mitigate instabilities, allowing large, sparse models to be trained with lower precision formats. Using models based on T5-Base and T5-Large, they achieved up to a 7x increase in pre-training speed without additional computational resources. These improvements also extended to multilingual settings, showing gains over the mT5-Base version across all 101 languages. Switch Transformer employs techniques like positional encoding, dynamic routing, and layer normalization to enhance performance. Dynamic routing, the core mechanism, directs each input token to the most relevant expert, improving context capture and reducing unnecessary computations. It handles various data types, making it ideal for NLP tasks like text generation, translation, and summarization and for analyzing sequential data over time, such as in financial forecasting and sensor data analysis. It is also adaptable for tasks involving structured data inputs, such as tables or categorical data." .


