###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SystemDesignAndUserInterfaceIssuesHazard
:SystemDesignAndUserInterfaceIssuesHazard rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                   :DataEntryAndCollection ;
                                          :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#IncreasedEntryErrors;UserDissatisfaction> ;
                                          :HasDetectability :LowDetectability ;
                                          :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
                                          :HasMitigation :HumanFactorsIntegration ,
                                                         :RiskMeasuresForUsability ,
                                                         :UsabilityEngineeringProcessIntegration ,
                                                         :UseRelatedRiskAnalysis ,
                                                         :UserErrorAnalysis ;
                                          :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PoorlyDesignedInterfaces;LackOfFeedback> ;
                                          :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
                                          :RegulatedBy <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC62366-1:2015Standard> ;
                                          :HasDetectabilityScore 7 ;
                                          :HasLikelihoodScore 8 ;
                                          :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
                                          rdfs:comment """The design of data entry systems and user interfaces plays a crucial role in preventing or causing hazards. Complex or non-intuitive interfaces can contribute to data entry errors. If a system lacks confirmation mechanisms or fails to provide adequate feedback, users may unknowingly submit incorrect data. Research highlights the importance of user-centered design in healthcare systems to minimize errors and enhance data accuracy, with poorly designed interfaces associated with increased user error and patient risk. IEC 62366-1:2015 requires a systematic approach to usability engineering for medical devices, such as Usability Engineering Process Integration for user specification and user interface evaluation, Use-Related Risk Analysis for critical tasks identification, user error analysis, and risk Measures for Usability, and Human Factors Integration for cognitive load assessment, environmental factors, and user characteristics.

The system design significantly impacts everything in healthcare settings, from usability to patient safety, and can lead to substantial risks if flawed. Masci et al. discussed the formal verification of medical device user interfaces, highlighting the potential consequences of interface issues on patient outcomes and the commonality of these errors due to complex human-machine interactions. The severity of system and interface design flaws can be high due to their potential to cause incorrect medical decisions or delays in treatment. In contrast, the likelihood of these errors is relatively common given the complexity and varied use environments of healthcare systems. The detectability of these issues can be challenging as they often require rigorous testing and user feedback, which are not always adequately performed in the developmental stages of medical device software.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SystemOutages
:SystemOutages rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        :EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazards ;
               :HasConsequence <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DataCorruption;ServiceDisruptions> ;
               :HasDetectability :HighDetectability ;
               :HasLikelihood :MediumLikelihood ;
               :HasMitigation :ComprehensiveDisasterRecoveryProtocols ,
                              :RegularServerMaintenance ,
                              :UninterruptiblePowerSupplies-UPS ;
               :HasPotentialCause <http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PowerFailures;ServerCrashes> ;
               :HasSeverity :HighSeverity ;
               :HasDetectabilityScore 2 ;
               :HasLikelihoodScore 5 ;
               :HasSeverityScore 9 ;
               rdfs:comment """System outages in healthcare can result from power failures, network disruptions, server crashes, or natural disasters, leading to significant disruptions in clinical services and data accessibility. Literature highlights several causes, such as insufficient power backups, overloaded servers, and a lack of disaster recovery plans. The consequences are severe, including data corruption, service disruptions, and delayed patient care. Mitigation strategies involve implementing uninterruptible power supplies (UPS), conducting regular server maintenance, and establishing comprehensive disaster recovery protocols to minimize downtime and data loss.

System outages in healthcare can directly disrupt critical operations such as patient monitoring and emergency procedures. Hospitals heavily depend on interconnected systems for clinical tasks, and outages could delay care (exemplified by the case of Hancock Regional Hospital, where a ransomware attack caused systems to go offline), potentially risking patient safety, which marks the severity of such failure is high. System outages can occur due to hardware failure, software errors, or external factors such as power outages or natural disasters. The likelihood of system outage is often medium; however, healthcare facilities usually implement redundancies, such as backup servers, to mitigate these risks. Outages can generally be detected quickly through network monitoring tools. Network monitoring tools and backup systems often enable the quick detection of system outages; however, identifying and resolving the underlying causes can take longer, especially in complex healthcare networks.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SystemPerformanceOptimisation
:SystemPerformanceOptimisation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :RealTimeDataProcessingFailuresMitigation .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/T-DM
:T-DM rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :DiffusionModels ;
      :HasModality :ImageData ;
      :UsesTechnique :CrossAttention ,
                     :DiscreteCosinesTransform ,
                     :LayerNormalisation ,
                     :LongSkipConnections ,
                     :Patchification ;
      rdfs:comment "Transformer-based Diffusion Models (T-DMs) leverage the transformer architecture to enhance the prediction and generation capabilities in various domains. These models transform input data into a more manageable frequency or latent space and treat all inputs as tokens for simplicity and efficiency. They aim to generate highquality, contextually accurate outputs, whether in human motion prediction, image generation, or medical image segmentation, by employing transformers for better scalability, efficiency, and handling of complex conditional dependencies. Several models fail to handle human motion’s uncertainty and multi-modal nature, producing unrealistic sequences. Authors developed a model, TransFusion, to address the challenge of predicting 3D human motion for Human-Robot Collaboration (HRC) in remanufacturing systems, aiming to balance accuracy and diversity in motion predictions. Their model leverages a Transformer-based architecture with long skip connections and utilizes the Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) to model motion sequences in the frequency domain. The model treats all inputs, including conditions and diffusion steps, as tokens, eliminating the need for extra modules like cross-attention. They performed extensive experiments on datasets such as Human3.6M, HumanEva-I, Archive of Mocap as Surface Shapes (AMASS), and a custom HRC dataset. They used the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.0003, batch size of 64, and 1000 diffusion steps. The model was trained for 1500 epochs on Human3.6M, 100 epochs on HumanEva-I, and 3000 epochs on AMASS, with 9, 5, and 13 Transformer layers, respectively, and a hidden state dimension of 512 for all datasets. Moreover, authors produced a MedSegDiff-V2 model to enhance medical image segmentation by integrating transformer mechanisms with Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DPM). Their developed framework employs two key techniques: Anchor Condition with Uncertain Spatial Attention (U-SA) to reduce diffusion variance by integrating conditional segmentation features into the diffusion model encoder and Semantic Condition with Spectrum-Space Transformer (SS-Former) to align noise and semantic features through cross-attention in the frequency domain using a Neural Band-pass Filter (NBP-Filter). To demonstrate the model’s effectiveness, they conducted experiments on various datasets, such as AMOS2022, Beyond The Cranial Vault (BTCV), Retinal Fundus Glaucoma Challenge Edition2 (REFUGE-2), Brain Tumor Segmentation (BraTs) challenge 2021, International Skin Imaging Collaboration (ISIC), and TNMIX. The model was trained using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.0001, a batch size 32, and 100 diffusion steps for inference, with results averaged over 10 ensemble runs. Transformer-based diffusion models mostly support image data, including 3D human motion image data. They can employ various advanced techniques, such as discrete cosine transform, SE-Transformer blocks, long skip connections, patchification, layer normalization, cross-attention, and specific mechanisms for handling conditional embeddings. These techniques enhance the models’ ability to handle diverse data types, improve computational efficiency, and generate high-quality predictions or segmentations." .


