###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TabularData
:TabularData rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      :StructuredData ;
             :GeneratedBy :ADSGAN ,
                          :BayesianNetworks ,
                          :CTGAN ,
                          :CopulaGAN ,
                          :DPCGAN ,
                          :DPGAN ,
                          :GReaT ,
                          :GaussianCopula ,
                          :GaussianMixtureModels-GMM ,
                          :GaussianMultivariate ,
                          :HealthGAN ,
                          :InfoGAN ,
                          :KDE ,
                          :LDP-GAN ,
                          :LSGAN ,
                          :LSTMGAN ,
                          :MIIC-SDG ,
                          :MedGAN ,
                          :PATEGAN ,
                          :PrivBayes ,
                          :RE-aLTabFormer ,
                          :RTSGAN ,
                          :SDV ,
                          :SequentialTreeSynthesis ,
                          :Synthpop ,
                          :TVAE ,
                          :TabDDPM ,
                          :TabSyn ,
                          :TabularPreset ,
                          :pGAN ,
                          :tGAN ;
             rdfs:comment "This data type is maintained in rows and columns. The columns represent different attributes or variables; each row contains a single record, sample, or observation. This data type is considered a straightforward method for data storage and analysis; however, generating synthetic tabular data is challenging due to multiple factors, such as semantic integrity in the records. Moreover, the features may have different data types and dependencies, such as discrete, continuous, and temporal. As an example, Electronic Health Records (EHRs) of patients are stored as tabular data. These records are stored in the structured form of tables or databases. Each row presents a unique visit of the patient, and columns contain different features relevant to that visit. These records may contain different information about the patient, such as name, gender, age, history, treatment, diagnosis, lab reports, demographics, and procedures. This data can be used for various purposes, such as clinical decision-making, research, and healthcare management. On the individual level, examples concern predicting trends and associations concerning the patient’s condition, demographics, and medications. On a more general level, an example is using EHRs to model patient length of stay." .


