###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TimeSeriesData
:TimeSeriesData rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         :StructuredData ;
                :GeneratedBy :ACGAN ,
                             :ADGAN ,
                             :ALAE ,
                             :BEGAN ,
                             :CGAN ,
                             :CoGAN ,
                             :DCGAN ,
                             :DGAN ,
                             :DPCTGAN ,
                             :DiscoGAN ,
                             :EBGAN ,
                             :EHR-M-GAN ,
                             :FewShotGAN ,
                             :FineGAN ,
                             :GluGAN ,
                             :HealthGAN ,
                             :MC-WGAN ,
                             :MWEM ,
                             :PATECTGAN ,
                             :ProcessGAN ,
                             :RTSGAN ,
                             :RandomizedGaussianSinusoidsAndFourierBasedNoiseModeling ,
                             :SAGAN ,
                             :SynTEG ,
                             :T2I ,
                             :TabGPT ,
                             :TransformerBasedContextGenerator ,
                             :VanillaGAN ,
                             :WGAN ,
                             :WGAN-GP ,
                             :WGAN-GP-GEOT-VAE-Buffer ;
                rdfs:comment "Time series data exhibit temporal dependencies and are usually presented in tabular form. However, time series and tabular data are often treated differently. Time-series data is collected or measured regularly, for example, in time intervals of minutes or hours. Next to the value, the data usually contains the time stamp of a measurement as well. Examples of time-series data in healthcare are vital signs like heart rate, temperature, respiratory rate, and blood pressure. Medical practitioners use this data to monitor patients’ conditions, follow trends, and detect anomalies. This data is crucial for clinical decision-making, analyzing patient health progression, treatment response, and timely interventions. Working with time series data presents multiple challenges. First, periodicity may be an issue when data is not sampled or measured at regular intervals. Second, capturing temporal patterns like autocorrelation can be difficult." .


