###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/VAEs
:VAEs rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :AutoencoderBasedGenerationModels ;
      rdfs:comment "Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) incorporate Bayesian inference to model the probability distribution of input data. Comprising an encoder, latent space, and decoder, VAEs effectively compress and reconstruct data, making them suitable for generative tasks like image, text, and audio synthesis. Their stability and capability to estimate probability distributions are advantageous for applications ranging from speech generation to biomedical informatics, enabling data denoising and new data sample generation. Authors address the challenge of improving machine learning performance with limited labeled data by employing VAEs and Conditional VAEs on the MNIST and Fashion-MNIST datasets. The goal was to use generative models to learn latent representations and condition outputs on additional information, enhancing the models’ ability to work with labeled and unlabeled data. The methodology involves training fully connected neural networks with ReLU activations and Gaussian latent spaces, optimizing the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO) to balance reconstruction quality and posterior regularization. Experiments were conducted using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.001, a batch size of 128, and training for 100 epochs. Network architectures included single hidden layers for MNIST and two hidden layers for Fashion-MNIST, with binarized input images and output modeled as Bernoulli distributions. Similarly, authors overcome the limitations of Semi-supervised Variational Autoencoders (SemiVAE) in text classification, particularly with vanilla Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) decoders. They proposed a Semi-supervised Sequential Variational Autoencoder (SSVAE), which enhances decoder performance by incorporating label information at each time step in the Recurrent Neural Network (RNN). Two Conditional LSTM (CLSTM) structures, such as CLSTM-I and CLSTM-II, were evaluated, with the latter showing better results. The model employs Adam optimizer with a 0.004 learning rate and includes techniques like cost annealing and word dropout. Experiments on nternet Movie Database (IMDb) and AG’s News corpus demonstrate significant improvements over pure-supervised LSTM models, with SSVAE-II showing superior performance. Moreover, authors introduced the VAE framework to model images and their associated labels using a Deep Generative Deconvolutional Network (DGDN) as a decoder and a CNN as an encoder. This setup facilitates efficient prediction and semi-supervised or unsupervised learning. The methodology integrates a Bayesian SVM for labels and a Gated Recurrent Unit for captions, optimizing parameters through variational learning with stochastic gradient descent. Experimental evaluations on datasets like MNIST, CIFAR-10/100, Caltech 101/256, ImageNet, and Microsoft Common Objects in COntext (MS COCO) demonstrate the model’s effectiveness. They employed an Adam optimizer with a learning rate 0.0002, a batch size 64, and gradient clipping when the parameter vector norm exceeds 5. VAEs support various data types, including images, text, audio, and time-series. They are effectively used in tasks such as image classification, image denoising, text classification (e.g., sentiment analysis and topic classification), and biomedical informatics involving medical images and biosignal applications such as Electroencephalogram (EEG), Electromyography (EMG) and Electrocardiogram (ECG). They leverage various advanced techniques to enhance their performance across different applications. Key techniques include the reparameterization trick, Bayesian variational inference, and various VAE variants such as β-VAE and Information Theoretic-Learning autoencoder (ITL-AE). In terms of optimization, VAEs employ variance reduction, Gibbs sampling, and Monte Carlo Expectation-Maximization (MCEM) to efficiently learn and infer model parameters." .


