###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ViT
:ViT rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
     :HasModality :ImageData ;
     :UsesTechnique :LayerNormalisation ,
                    :PatchEmbedding ,
                    :PositionalEncoding ;
     rdfs:comment "The Vision Transformer (ViT) revolutionizes computer vision tasks by replacing traditional convolutional layers with self-attention mechanisms, allowing the model to capture long-range dependencies within image data more effectively. ViT’s comprehensive documentation details its innovative architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics, ensuring transparency and reproducibility in its application across various computer vision tasks. It processes images by dividing them into fixed-size patches, which are then linearly embedded and combined with positional encodings. This sequence of embeddings is fed into a standard Transformer model, originally designed for NLP but can be adapted for image data. Authors surveyed the applications of Vision Transformers in medical computer vision, covering areas like image-based disease classification, anatomical structure segmentation, registration, lesion detection, captioning, report generation, and reconstruction across various medical imaging modalities. These applications significantly aid in medical diagnosis and treatment. They also explained several imaging modalities used in medical computer vision and provided insights into the self-attention mechanism of transformers for a deeper understanding. CNNs used for ECG analysis typically need large datasets, and transfer learning from natural images can lead to suboptimal results. Authors used masked image modeling to develop a vision-based transformer model for ECG waveform analysis. They pre-trained the model on 8.5 million ECGs and compared its performance with standard CNNs for diagnosing hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, low left ventricular ejection fraction, and ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Using the AdamW optimizer with a 0.0005 learning rate, they found that their model performed significantly better at lower sample sizes. This approach allows for more accurate and granular explainability of model predictions. ViT enhances model performance through positional encoding, patch embedding, and layer normalization. Positional encoding captures spatial relationships between patches, patch embedding transforms image patches into fixed-dimensional vectors for self-attention, and layer normalization stabilizes the training process. ViT excels in computer vision tasks such as image classification, object detection, and image segmentation, effectively handling high-resolution and complex images. It is adaptable to healthcare applications like analyzing MRI, CT scans, and X-rays for precise image classification and segmentation, and is also useful for remote sensing applications like land cover classification and change detection." .


