###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WGAN
:WGAN rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :GANs ;
      :GeneratesModality :ImageData ,
                         :TextData ,
                         :TimeSeriesData ;
      :UsesTechnique :FeatureMatching ,
                     :LatentSpaceEncodingAdjustmentManipulationInterpolationRegularisation ,
                     :OneHotEncoding ;
      rdfs:comment """The Wasserstein GAN (WGAN) leverages the Wasserstein distance as its loss function to improve training stability and generate high-quality, diverse datasets. This approach addresses common issues in traditional GAN training, such as mode collapse and vanishing gradients, by providing a smoother and more informative
gradient signal. WGAN includes comprehensive documentation of its architecture, training procedures, and evaluation metrics to ensure reproducibility and transparency. WGAN supports various data types, including image, text, and time series data. WGAN typically uses learning rates between 0.00005 and 0.0001 to stabilize training and ensure steady convergence and primarily employs the RMSprop optimizer due to its effectiveness in handling the gradient clipping required by the Wasserstein distance. Authors trained the WGAN model by employing a learning rate of 0.00005 for RMSProp and 0.0001 for SGD with a batch size of 64 for improving the semantic fine-grained classification for lung nodules in CT images. Similarly, authors used the Adam optimizer with the learning rate 0.0002 to train their model on batch size 64, and they followed this up by training an ensemble of 5 generators using SGD to evaluate the reproducibility of the results on CIFA-10 dataset.""" .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WGAN-GP
:WGAN-GP rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :GANs .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WGAN-GP-GEOT-VAE-Buffer
:WGAN-GP-GEOT-VAE-Buffer rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  :GANs .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WassersteinDistance
:WassersteinDistance rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              :DiversityMetrics ,
                              :StatisticalDistanceMeasures ;
                     :HasModality :TabularData ;
                     rdfs:comment "Also referred to as Earth’s Mover distance, Wasserstein distance is another measure to quantify the distance between two probability distributions. It can be interpreted as the cost of transforming one distribution function into the other by moving distribution mass over the required distance. Wasserstein distance satisfies all properties of a proper distance metric. Wasserstein distance has become popular in generative modeling to alleviate the vanishing gradient problem when training GANs. This can lead to higher quality of generated samples." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WaveletTransform
:WaveletTransform rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/WeightAveraging
:WeightAveraging rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          :Techniques .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/XLNet
:XLNet rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                :TransformerBasedGenerativeModels ;
       :HasModality :DialogueData ,
                    :TextData ;
       :UsesTechnique :AutoregressiveDecoding ,
                      :BidirectionalModeling ,
                      :PermutationBasedModeling ,
                      :TransformerXLArchitecture ;
       rdfs:comment "XLNet enhances language modeling by implementing a permutation-based objective that captures bidirectional context, thereby improving text generation and understanding across various NLP tasks. XLNet’s documentation provides detailed information on its architecture, training methodologies, and evaluation metrics, ensuring clarity and reproducibility in model development. Authors investigated advanced deep learning techniques to predict mortality risk in cardiovascular disease patients using EHR data from over 23,000 cardiac patients. They compared BERT and XLNet models, formulating patient histories as time series of clinical events to capture complex temporal dependencies. BERT and XLNet configurations included 108,312,578 and 5,482,130 trainable parameters, respectively, and were trained using the Adam optimizer with batch sizes of 16 and 64. Optimal learning rates for BERT were around 0.0000005 to 0.000001, while XLNet performed best at 0.000005 to 0.00001. XLNet outperformed BERT in the recall by 9.8%, indicating it captured more positive cases. XLNet employs unique techniques, including permutation-based modeling, Transformer-XL architecture, bidirectional context modeling, and auto-regressive decoding, to enhance model performance [268]. Its permutation-based modeling captures bidirectional context without BERT’s independence assumption, while Transformer-XL’s segment-level recurrence and relative positional encoding handle long-term dependencies in long text sequences. Unlike traditional models, XLNet captures context from all directions, improving text understanding, and auto-regressive decoding ensures coherent and contextually relevant text generation. XLNet’s versatility makes it effective for tasks like text classification, sentiment analysis, summarization, generating descriptions from structured datasets, and dialogue systems, where contextually appropriate responses are crucial." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/aPrecisionbRecall
:aPrecisionbRecall rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                            :AccuracyMetrics ,
                            :FidelityMetrics ,
                            :PrivacyMetrics ,
                            :StatisticalDistanceMeasures ;
                   :HasModality :TabularData ,
                                :TimeSeriesData ;
                   rdfs:comment "Extending the work of introduces α-Precision and β-Recall as sample-level evaluation metrics for generative models. Here, α-Precision indicates whether a generated sample falls within the α-support of the actual distribution, whereas β-Recall indicates whether an actual sample falls within the Beta-support of the learned distribution. Hereby they consider specific parts of the support of distributions instead of the entire support, where specific parts of the support relate to more or less densely sampled regions of the data space. This way, they consider support and the likelihood of samples. Specific parts of the support that relate to their likelihood are made possible by embedding samples into a hypersphere, with typical samples near the center and outliers near the edges. The α-Precision and β-Recall analyses have been used widely to assess generative models" ,
                                "With Authenticity - to assess not only fidelity, but also diversity andgeneralization performance." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/bRecall
:bRecall rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                  :DiversityMetrics ;
         :HasModality :TabularData .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/eDifferentialPrivacy
:eDifferentialPrivacy rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               :PrivacyAgainstInference ;
                      rdfs:comment """Differential privacy was developed as a privacy-preserving framework with the
aim of quantifying the maximum contribution of individual data points to the output of an algorithm and originates in the work of. In an ϵ-differentially private algorithm, the likelihood ratio of the algorithms’ output, when using either the entire dataset or the entire dataset minus a single datapoint, is at most e^ϵ. Here, ϵ is self-specified. The contribution of an individual datapoint is typically controlled by adding noise/perturbation to ensure privacy at the cost of downstream task performance. Hence, another way to think about ϵ is as the level of indistinguishability: adding noise (higher ϵ) makes an individual contribution less distinguishable, increasing privacy, but may also obscure the signal for a particular task. Different approaches exist to ensure a private synthetic dataset for injecting noise during the training phase, for example, by clipping or injecting noise in the gradients in training an autoencoder. These approaches have been employed in generating data for various health datasets concerning breast cancer and diabetes. However, other approaches exist that draw, e.g., on Private Teacher Ensemble-GANS and partitioning of training data on which different teacher networks are trained, and DP-CTGAN that makes use of a conditional generator.""" .


